{"type":"agent_end","messages":[{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are an expert coding assistant operating inside pi, a coding agent harness. You help users by reading files, executing commands, editing code, and writing new files.","tools":"<tools>\n- read: Read file contents\n- bash: Execute bash commands (ls, grep, find, etc.)\n- edit: Make precise file edits with exact text replacement, including multiple disjoint edits in one call\n- write: Create or overwrite files\n\nIn addition to the tools above, you may have access to other custom tools depending on the project.\n</tools>","rules":"<rules>\n- Use bash for file operations like ls, rg, find\n- Use read to examine files instead of cat or sed.\n- You can inspect PI_* environment variables for current model and session details.\n- Use edit for precise changes (edits[].oldText must match exactly)\n- When changing multiple separate locations in one file, use one edit call with multiple entries in edits[] instead of multiple edit calls\n- Each edits[].oldText is matched against the original file, not after earlier edits are applied. Do not emit overlapping or nested edits. Merge nearby changes into one edit.\n- Keep edits[].oldText as small as possible while still being unique in the file. Do not pad with large unchanged regions.\n- Use write only for new files or complete rewrites.\n- Be concise in your responses\n- Show file paths clearly when working with files\n</rules>","docs":"<docs>\nPi documentation (read only when the user asks about pi itself, its SDK, extensions, themes, skills, or TUI):\n- Main documentation: /usr/local/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/README.md\n- Additional docs: /usr/local/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs\n- Examples: /usr/local/lib/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/examples (extensions, custom tools, SDK)\n- When reading pi docs or examples, resolve docs/... under Additional docs and examples/... under Examples, not the current working directory\n- When asked about: extensions (docs/extensions.md, examples/extensions/), themes (docs/themes.md), skills (docs/skills.md), prompt templates (docs/prompt-templates.md), TUI components (docs/tui.md), keybindings (docs/keybindings.md), SDK integrations (docs/sdk.md), custom providers (docs/custom-provider.md), adding models (docs/models.md), pi packages (docs/packages.md), environment variables (docs/environment-variables.md), MCP servers (docs/mcp.md), codemode scripts and non-LLM models such as classifiers and image models (docs/codemode.md)\n- When working on pi topics, read the docs and examples, and follow .md cross-references before implementing\n- Always read pi .md files completely and follow links to related docs (e.g., tui.md for TUI API details)\n</docs>","skills":"<skills>\nThe following skills provide specialized instructions for specific tasks.\nUse the read tool to load a skill's file when the task matches its description.\nWhen a skill file references a relative path, resolve it against the skill directory (parent of SKILL.md / dirname of the path) and use that absolute path in tool commands.\n\n<available_skills>\n  <skill>\n    <name>admission-rubric</name>\n    <description>Applies a four-step admission and verification check to a finished task&apos;s diff before it is allowed to count as done. Use only when reviewing work produced by a different agent instance, never to review your own output, during Testing and Verification.</description>\n    <location>/opt/watts/kit/skills/admission-rubric/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>plan-template</name>\n    <description>Produces PLAN.md from the bundled template once the spec-review workflow gate is approved. Use during Design and Architecture to define a technical approach, data model, risk tier recheck, quality gates, and the user stories the build loop will work through.</description>\n    <location>/opt/watts/kit/skills/plan-template/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>retrospective-log</name>\n    <description>Writes a structured retrospective entry after a task reaches Deployment and Release, or is rejected and abandoned. Use at the end of any Tier 1 or Tier 2 task, or any Tier 0 task that failed in an unexpected way.</description>\n    <location>/opt/watts/kit/skills/retrospective-log/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>risk-tiering</name>\n    <description>Classifies a task into Tier 0, 1, or 2 based on what it touches in this team&apos;s systems. Use during Planning and Requirements to assign the initial tier, and again during Design and during Testing and Verification to recheck it.</description>\n    <location>/opt/watts/kit/skills/risk-tiering/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>spec-template</name>\n    <description>Draft or refine a task&apos;s SPEC.md from human intent, project evidence, and review feedback before implementation planning.</description>\n    <location>/opt/watts/kit/skills/spec-template/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n  <skill>\n    <name>go-builder</name>\n    <description>Implement and test Go business application and web service changes in the Watts Go generalist workflow, following the approved spec, plan, and team lint policy.</description>\n    <location>/inputs/resources/team/skills/go-builder/SKILL.md</location>\n  </skill>\n</available_skills>\n</skills>","cwd":"<cwd>\n/workspace\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1791815345139,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"edit","description":"Edit a single file using exact text replacement. Every edits[].oldText must match a unique, non-overlapping region of the original file. If two changes affect the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead of emitting overlapping edits. Do not include large unchanged regions just to connect distant changes.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","edits"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to edit (relative or absolute)"},"edits":{"type":"array","items":{"type":"object","required":["oldText","newText"],"properties":{"oldText":{"type":"string","description":"Exact text for one targeted replacement. It must be unique in the original file and must not overlap with any other edits[].oldText in the same call."},"newText":{"type":"string","description":"Replacement text for this targeted edit."}}},"description":"One or more targeted replacements. Each edit is matched against the original file, not incrementally. Do not include overlapping or nested edits. If two changes touch the same block or nearby lines, merge them into one edit instead."}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"write","description":"Write content to a file. Creates the file if it doesn't exist, overwrites if it does. Automatically creates parent directories.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path","content"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to write (relative or absolute)"},"content":{"type":"string","description":"Content to write to the file"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}}]},{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read the human's request in /runtime/task/SPEC.md; it may be rough notes rather than a complete draft. Use the spec-template skill to investigate the project and draft or refine /runtime/task/SPEC.md. Preserve existing human intent, constraints, and substantive content; never overwrite it with a blank template. Record unresolved questions rather than inventing requirements. Incorporate previous review feedback. Write the updated SPEC.md to disk. Do not implement code or approve documents. The application workspace is the current working directory supplied by Watts; the package descriptor may live elsewhere. Implement application source, tests, modules, and other project files in the project root and its normal source directories. The task directory holds workflow documents, story logs, review artifacts, and execution evidence only; do not create application code or a separate application module inside it. Quality gates run from the project root, not with a task-directory working directory.\nTask documents are in /runtime/task. Application work belongs in /workspace. Write the requested task outputs to /runtime/task."}],"timestamp":1791815345139},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me start by reading the human's request in SPEC.md and the spec-template skill to understand what I need to do.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Let me start by reading the human's request in SPEC.md and the spec-template skill to understand what I need to do.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f13892bafae1485ca5d150df","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/runtime/task/SPEC.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_0f0daf85a3fd46d5a5a4b0cf","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/opt/watts/kit/skills/spec-template/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":2380,"output":119,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":25,"totalTokens":2499,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791815345251,"responseId":"gen-1791815345-T8qms99VzJIwDtfDUgca","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_f13892bafae1485ca5d150df","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Tic-tac-toe\n\nCreate a minimal terminal tic-tac-toe game in Go for two local human players. Use only the Go standard library. Players X and O alternate selecting cells 1–9. Print the board and whose turn it is. Reject occupied cells, out-of-range moves, and nonnumeric input without changing turns. Detect all rows, columns, diagonals, and draws; announce the result and stop. Exit cleanly on EOF. No AI opponent, web server, persistence, configuration framework, or external application dependencies. Keep the implementation small and readable, preferably one main.go plus focused tests. Create go.mod and application files in the project root, not the task directory. Verify wins, draws, invalid moves, and EOF with meaningful tests. Run go test ./... and the configured lint gate.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815349350},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_0f0daf85a3fd46d5a5a4b0cf","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: spec-template\ndescription: Draft or refine a task's SPEC.md from human intent, project evidence, and review feedback before implementation planning.\n---\n\n# Draft a software specification\n\nTurn the human's request into a clear, reviewable `SPEC.md`. A rough request is enough to begin. The human supplies intent; the agent drafts the specification.\n\n## Preserve the request\n\n- Read the existing task `SPEC.md` before editing it.\n- Preserve human intent, constraints, non-goals, and substantive content when reorganizing the draft.\n- Use [spec-template.md](spec-template.md) as a structure reference. Copy it only if `SPEC.md` does not exist; never replace an existing draft with a blank template.\n- If the file contains only boilerplate, record the missing intent under Open Questions and ask the human for their request. Do not invent a task.\n- Write only in the selected task directory, never at the project root or in another task.\n\n## Drafting checklist\n\n1. **Establish the purpose.** Identify the affected user, their problem, and the observable result they need. Preserve the meaning of the human's request.\n2. **Inspect the project.** Read relevant code and documentation. Describe current behavior and cite relevant project paths. Separate observed facts, assumptions, and unanswered questions.\n3. **Bound the scope.** State what the change includes and excludes. Carry forward supplied constraints. Do not add adjacent features simply because they seem useful.\n4. **Specify behavior.** Write numbered acceptance criteria for the requested behavior, including relevant failure cases and boundary conditions. Every criterion must be verifiable.\n5. **Expose uncertainty.** Put unresolved decisions under Open Questions, explaining what needs an answer. Do not silently turn guesses into requirements or invent thresholds.\n6. **Assess risk.** Use the `risk-tiering` skill to assign the tier from the actual affected behavior.\n7. **Edit for clarity.** Apply the writing checklist below, then save the completed draft to `SPEC.md`.\n\n`SPEC.md` explains what the system must do and why. Implementation approach, code structure, story sequencing, and test execution belong in `PLAN.md`. Keep human-mandated technical constraints in the spec, but do not invent an implementation there.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nUse `- AC-1: ...`, `- AC-2: ...`, and so on. Give each criterion one observable response and a concrete system actor. Choose the EARS pattern that fits the behavior:\n\n| Behavior | Pattern |\n| --- | --- |\n| Always required | The system shall perform the response. |\n| Triggered by an event | When the event occurs, the system shall perform the response. |\n| Applies during a state | While the state holds, the system shall perform the response. |\n| Handles unwanted behavior | If the unwanted condition occurs, then the system shall perform the response. |\n| Depends on an optional feature | Where the feature is enabled, the system shall perform the response. |\n\nCombine a state and event when both matter. Do not force every criterion into the same pattern.\n\nFor example:\n\n- AC-1: When the user selects a task, the monitor shall display that task's workflow stages.\n- AC-2: If the execution service cannot answer a status query, then the monitor shall identify the task's execution status as unavailable.\n\nReplace vague claims such as “fast,” “robust,” or “user-friendly” with observable behavior or a human-supplied measurable target. If a target is missing, record the question. The plan must map its stories to these criterion IDs.\n\n## Writing checklist\n\n- Lead with the purpose and write for the human who will approve the work.\n- Use consistent names for users, components, and actions. Define unfamiliar terms when first used.\n- Prefer active voice with an explicit actor. Replace ambiguous pronouns with the component's name.\n- Keep sentences short and paragraphs focused on one topic.\n- Use headings to separate concerns, lists for parallel items, and tables for comparisons.\n- Remove repeated statements, template instructions, and unfilled placeholders from the draft.\n- Keep facts, assumptions, constraints, and questions distinguishable.\n\n## Save and review\n\nUse the bundled template's headings and metadata format:\n\n| Field | Required format |\n| --- | --- |\n| Status | `Draft` or `In Review` on the line below `## Status`; agents never mark their own work approved. |\n| Version | `Version: vN` in the Status section. |\n| Risk tier | A single `0`, `1`, or `2` on the line below `## Risk tier`. |\n| Acceptance criteria | Numbered `- AC-N: ...` entries with substantive content. |\n| Explicit non-goals | Concrete scope exclusions rather than template filler. |\n| Open questions | Actual unresolved decisions, or an explicit statement that none remain. |\n\nThe default workflow pauses at `spec-review`. Human approval is recorded by the workflow gate against the document hash; a signature in the document does not approve the workflow. Never sign for a human or approve your own output.\n\nOn rejection, revise the existing draft using the feedback while preserving the original intent. Follow-up work on a completed task belongs in a new task folder; reference the preceding specification in `Supersedes`. Do not create a new task merely to revise a draft during its review loop.\n\n## Sources\n\nThis checklist condenses the following guidance into Watts' existing spec and plan workflow. It does not require installing their tools.\n\n- [GitHub Spec Kit](https://github.com/github/spec-kit), MIT: separate what and why from technical planning.\n- [Microsoft EARS acceptance guidance](https://github.com/microsoft/hve-core/blob/main/.github/skills/project-planning/requirements-author/references/prd/ears-acceptance.md), CC BY 4.0: select a suitable sentence pattern and specify observable behavior. EARS originates with Alistair Mavin, Philip Wilkinson, Adrian Harwood, and Mark Novak (2009).\n- [Google Technical Writing One](https://developers.google.com/tech-writing/one), CC BY 4.0: write for the reader using clear structure, consistent terminology, and concise active sentences.\n\nThe guidance above is adapted and paraphrased for Watts; examples are specific to Watts.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815349352},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to read the spec-template.md to understand the template structure, and also investigate the project. Let me look at the template and explore the workspace.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Now I need to read the spec-template.md to understand the template structure, and also investigate the project. Let me look at the template and explore the workspace.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_676280acda2b4848b75a81cc","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/opt/watts/kit/skills/spec-template/spec-template.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_99b65fdbf04542528b565862","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls -la /workspace/"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_4712346f49b04c2f89e1b19e","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"find /workspace -name \"go.mod\" -o -name \"*.go\" -o -name \"Makefile\" -o -name \".golangci.yml\" -o -name \"golangci*.yml\" 2>/dev/null | head -30"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":1678,"output":210,"cacheRead":2304,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":32,"totalTokens":4192,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791815349359,"responseId":"gen-1791815349-i8gx4UOQ4XiwXVAeOrUp","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_676280acda2b4848b75a81cc","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# SPEC: <name>\n\n## Status\nDraft / In Review / Approved / Superseded\nVersion: v1\nSupersedes: <none, or tasks/<name>/SPEC.md vN>\nOwner: <human name>\nLast updated: <date>\n\n## Intent\nWhy this exists, what problem it solves, for whom. One or two paragraphs, not a restatement of the acceptance criteria.\n\n## Background and context\nRelevant prior art, related systems, why this is being done now rather than earlier or later.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\nWrite each criterion using the EARS pattern that fits: an invariant, event, state, unwanted condition, or optional feature followed by the system response. Combine a state and event only when both matter. Not \"handles errors gracefully,\" that's not testable. Number each one (AC-1, AC-2, ...) so PLAN.md's user stories can name which criteria they cover. Example:\n\n- AC-1: While the queue is non-empty, when a worker polls, the dispatcher shall return exactly one unclaimed task.\n- AC-2: While no tasks are available, when a worker polls, the dispatcher shall return an empty result within 200ms, not block.\n\n- AC-3:\n\n## Explicit non-goals\nWhat this deliberately does not do. Required, not optional, this is what the admission rubric checks a diff against for invented scope.\n\n-\n\n## Assumptions\nWhat's being taken as given. Worth stating explicitly, because if one of these is wrong, the spec is wrong, not the implementation.\n\n-\n\n## Constraints\nTechnical, regulatory, timeline, or resourcing limits that shape the acceptance criteria above.\n\n-\n\n## Open questions\nAmbiguity that has to be resolved by a human before PLAN.md gets written, not silently assumed by whoever implements this. If this section is empty, that's worth a second look, not a sign everything's clear.\n\n-\n\n## Risk-relevant context\n- Touches:\n- Does not touch:\n\n## Risk tier\n<0 / 1 / 2, from the risk-tiering skill>\n\n## Review\n\nAn agent drafts this document. The human approves or rejects it at the spec-review workflow gate with `wctl task decide <task> spec-review`, optionally using `--reject --feedback 'changes needed'`.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815353924},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_99b65fdbf04542528b565862","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 4\ndrwx------ 2 root root   64 Oct 12 14:29 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 root root 4096 Oct 12 14:29 ..\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815353925},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_4712346f49b04c2f89e1b19e","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(no output)"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815353927},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The workspace is empty - no existing Go project. The user's request is clear and well-written. Let me also check the risk-tiering skill since the spec-template skill says to use it for risk assessment.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"The workspace is empty - no existing Go project. The user's request is clear and well-written. Let me also check the risk-tiering skill since the spec-template skill says to use it for risk assessment.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_ab2026e1854548edb6460587","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/opt/watts/kit/skills/risk-tiering/SKILL.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_1d2d1e0f92d640939c70354b","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls -la /runtime/task/"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":901,"output":138,"cacheRead":3840,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":42,"totalTokens":4879,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791815353931,"responseId":"gen-1791815354-TkWpJcTfRXGj2GNuZpZm","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_ab2026e1854548edb6460587","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: risk-tiering\ndescription: Classifies a task into Tier 0, 1, or 2 based on what it touches in this team's systems. Use during Planning and Requirements to assign the initial tier, and again during Design and during Testing and Verification to recheck it.\n---\n\n# Risk Tiering\n\nRisk tiers guide review depth. Workflow definitions declare approval policy explicitly; the default SDLC has specification and plan review gates at every tier.\n\nThree tiers. Assign based on what the change actually touches, not how large or small the diff looks.\n\n## Tier 0, low risk\n\nDependency bumps. Documentation changes. Non-functional refactors and formatting. UI-only changes to a dashboard component itself, not the data it reads.\n\n## Tier 1, checkpoint logged before merge\n\nGovernance field value changes in a model or service registry. Cache or fallback chain tuning in a routing layer. Non-security pipeline stages, response formatting, post-processing. Protocol adapters that don't touch authentication.\n\n## Tier 2, human approval gate before execution starts\n\nAnything touching JWT claim validation or issuance, anything resembling model or resource access-control claims, budget attribution headers. Admission policy logic itself, not per-org configuration data, the policy. Entitlement primitive logic and hard limit enforcement. Planning-phase logic that produces an execution plan gating upstream calls. Identity or provisioning logic. Anything resembling credential handling.\n\n## The general rule for anything not listed above\n\n- If a mistake could let one identity spend or access what another identity is entitled to: Tier 2.\n- If a mistake could silently change routing or behavior without violating an entitlement: Tier 1.\n- If a mistake is cosmetic, immediately visible, and trivially reverted: Tier 0.\n\n## When to recheck\n\nAssign the initial tier during Planning and Requirements. Recheck it during Design, once the actual shape of the solution is known. Recheck it again during Testing and Verification if anything was discovered mid-task that changes the picture. A risk tier can move up mid-task. It never moves down silently.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815359848},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_1d2d1e0f92d640939c70354b","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 4\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 501 dialout  96 Oct 12 14:29 .\ndrwx------ 7 501 dialout 224 Oct 12 14:29 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 501 dialout 787 Oct 12 14:29 SPEC.md\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815359849},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me draft a refined SPEC.md.\n\nThe human's request is actually quite well-formed already. Let me assess:\n\n1. **Purpose**: A minimal terminal tic-tac-toe game in Go for two local human players.\n2. **Project inspection**: The workspace is empty - no existing Go project or code. So this is a new project.\n3. **Scope**: Clear boundaries - no AI, no web server, no persistence, no config framework, no external deps.\n4. **Acceptance criteria**: Need to write proper EARS-format criteria.\n5. **Risk**: This is a Tier 0 task - it's a local terminal game with no network, no auth, no entitlements. A mistake would be cosmetic/trivial.\n6. **Non-goals**: Already well-specified by the human.\n\nLet me draft the SPEC.md following the template structure while preserving all the human's intent and constraints.\n\nThe human already has quite specific requirements:\n- Two local human players (X and O)\n- Alternating turns\n- Cells 1-9\n- Print board and whose turn\n- Reject occupied, out-of-range, nonnumeric moves without changing turns\n- Detect wins (rows, columns, diagonals), draws\n- Announce result, stop\n- Exit cleanly on EOF\n- No AI, web server, persistence, config, external deps\n- One main.go + focused tests\n- go.mod in project root\n- Verify wins, draws, invalid moves, EOF with tests\n- Run go test ./... and lint gate\n\nLet me write the refined specification.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Now I have all the information I need. Let me draft a refined SPEC.md.\\n\\nThe human's request is actually quite well-formed already. Let me assess:\\n\\n1. **Purpose**: A minimal terminal tic-tac-toe game in Go for two local human players.\\n2. **Project inspection**: The workspace is empty - no existing Go project or code. So this is a new project.\\n3. **Scope**: Clear boundaries - no AI, no web server, no persistence, no config framework, no external deps.\\n4. **Acceptance criteria**: Need to write proper EARS-format criteria.\\n5. **Risk**: This is a Tier 0 task - it's a local terminal game with no network, no auth, no entitlements. A mistake would be cosmetic/trivial.\\n6. **Non-goals**: Already well-specified by the human.\\n\\nLet me draft the SPEC.md following the template structure while preserving all the human's intent and constraints.\\n\\nThe human already has quite specific requirements:\\n- Two local human players (X and O)\\n- Alternating turns\\n- Cells 1-9\\n- Print board and whose turn\\n- Reject occupied, out-of-range, nonnumeric moves without changing turns\\n- Detect wins (rows, columns, diagonals), draws\\n- Announce result, stop\\n- Exit cleanly on EOF\\n- No AI, web server, persistence, config, external deps\\n- One main.go + focused tests\\n- go.mod in project root\\n- Verify wins, draws, invalid moves, EOF with tests\\n- Run go test ./... and lint gate\\n\\nLet me write the refined specification.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Now I have everything I need. The human's request is quite detailed already. Let me draft the refined SPEC.md, preserving all existing intent while structuring it properly.\n\n"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_69d64a7bd5af4afbbea43e14","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/runtime/task/SPEC.md","content":"# Tic-tac-toe — minimal terminal game for two human players\n\n## Status\nDraft\nVersion: v1\nOwner: <to be assigned>\nLast updated: <date>\n\n## Intent\nTwo local human players need a terminal-based tic-tac-toe game they can run without installing anything beyond Go. The game prints the board each turn, accepts moves by cell number, enforces the rules, and announces the outcome — a win, a draw, or an abandoned session (EOF) — then stops. No AI, web server, persistence, or configuration framework is needed.\n\n## Background and context\nThe workspace is empty; no Go project exists yet. This is a greenfield implementation of one of the simplest possible interactive terminal games. The human request supplies all the rules, constraints, and scope boundaries needed. The implementation is expected to be a single `main.go` plus focused test files, with a `go.mod` at the project root. A lint gate (configured elsewhere) will run alongside `go test ./...`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- AC-1: When the program starts, the system shall print an empty 3×3 board labelled with cell numbers 1–9 and prompt Player X for a move.\n- AC-2: When a player enters a valid unoccupied cell number (1–9), the system shall place that player's mark, update the board display, and switch to the other player's turn.\n- AC-3: If a player enters a cell number that is already occupied, then the system shall print an error message, display the current board again, and keep the same player's turn without changing the board state.\n- AC-4: If a player enters a number outside the range 1–9, then the system shall print an error message and prompt the same player again without changing the board state.\n- AC-5: If a player enters nonnumeric input (text, special characters, etc.), then the system shall print an error message and prompt the same player again without changing the board state.\n- AC-6: After any move, if the board contains three matching marks in any row, column, or diagonal, the system shall announce the winning player and exit.\n- AC-7: After any move, if the board is full with no win, the system shall announce a draw and exit.\n- AC-8: When the program reads EOF (e.g., Ctrl+D / Ctrl+Z) at any input prompt, the system shall print an exit message and terminate cleanly with a zero exit code.\n- AC-9: When the program runs `go test ./...`, all tests shall pass.\n- AC-10: When the configured lint gate runs against the project, it shall report zero errors.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n\n- No AI opponent or computer player.\n- No network, web server, or API.\n- No persistence (file storage, database, leaderboard, save/load).\n- No configuration files, environment variables, or CLI flags beyond what Go's standard library supplies.\n- No external application dependencies beyond the Go standard library.\n- No concurrency, goroutines, or channels.\n- No GUI, ncurses, or interactive TUI framework — terminal line I/O only.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- The players are co-located at the same terminal and take turns at the same keyboard.\n- The `go.mod` file will declare the module path `tic-tac-toe` (or a name specified by the developer).\n- A configured linter (e.g., `golangci-lint`) is expected to pass; no specific linter config is supplied in this spec.\n- Go is already installed in the build environment.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Use only the Go standard library; no third-party packages.\n- The implementation must be readable and small — ideally one `main.go` plus separate test files.\n- Application files (`go.mod`, `*.go`) must be created in the project root (`/workspace`), not in the task directory (`/runtime/task`).\n- Zero lint errors per the project's configured lint gate.\n\n## Open questions\n\nNone. The human request, the acceptance criteria above, and the explicit non-goals cover all known decisions. Cell display format (e.g., ASCII grid with `|` and `-`) and exact error-message wording are left to the implementation, as they do not affect correctness.\n\n## Risk-relevant context\n\n- Touches: A new, standalone Go terminal application with no network access, no file I/O beyond terminal stdin/stdout, and no external dependencies.\n- Does not touch: Any existing production code, credentials, entitlements, identity, network services, persistent storage, or configuration infrastructure.\n\n## Risk tier\n0\n\n## Review\n\nAn agent drafts this document. The human approves or rejects it at the spec-review workflow gate with `wctl task decide <task> spec-review`, optionally using `--reject --feedback 'changes needed'`."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":783,"output":1458,"cacheRead":4608,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":337,"totalTokens":6849,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791815359852,"responseId":"gen-1791815359-W7w2ceqsxkyg3zWTBEzy","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_69d64a7bd5af4afbbea43e14","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote to /runtime/task/SPEC.md"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791815390951},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me verify the final file looks correct.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Let me verify the final file looks correct.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Let me verify the final output"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_745b963ce107455b8dd61520","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/runtime/task/SPEC.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":1495,"output":65,"cacheRead":5376,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":9,"totalTokens":6936,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791815390954,"responseId":"gen-1791815391-5YAPuMk9ON4RgGxHN7HY","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_745b963ce107455b8dd61520","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Tic-tac-toe — minimal terminal game for two human players\n\n## Status\nDraft\nVersion: v1\nOwner: <to be assigned>\nLast updated: <date>\n\n## Intent\nTwo local human players need a terminal-based tic-tac-toe game they can run without installing anything beyond Go. The game prints the board each turn, accepts moves by cell number, e