t me think about what's small enough for one iteration:\n\nUS-001: Project setup + board display (AC-1) — very straightforward\nUS-002: Valid move + input validation + board update (AC-2, AC-3, AC-4, AC-5) — core game loop\nUS-003: Win detection (AC-6) — algorithmic, riskiest\nUS-004: Draw detection (AC-7) — simple check after win\nUS-005: EOF handling (AC-8) — simple\nUS-006: Tests + lint (AC-9, AC-10) — writing the test files and making them pass\n\nActually, the plan says \"Put the riskiest or approach-proving story first\". The riskiest story is probably the win detection or the game loop. But the scaffolding is needed first.\n\nLet me refine:\n\nUS-001: Project scaffolding and board rendering — go.mod, main structure, print empty board (AC-1)\nUS-002: Turn management and move input — accept valid cell numbers, place marks, switch turns (AC-2), validate occupied cells (AC-3), out-of-range (AC-4), non-numeric (AC-5)\nUS-003: Win detection — detect all win conditions, announce winner, exit (AC-6)\nUS-004: Draw detection and EOF handling — detect full board (AC-7), handle EOF (AC-8)\nUS-005: Test suite and lint compliance — write all test files, ensure tests pass and lint is clean (AC-9, AC-10)\n\nThis seems reasonable. US-001 is approach-proving (shows Go project compiles and runs), US-002 is the core game loop logic, US-003 is algorithmic (win detection is the most complex logic), US-004 wraps up game-end conditions, and US-005 polishes with tests and lint.\n\nNow let me write the PLAN.md.\n\nFor quality gates, the instructions say to include `bash /inputs/resources/team/scripts/lint.sh`. Let me put:\n- `go build ./...`\n- `go vet ./...`\n- `go test ./...`\n- `bash /inputs/resources/team/scripts/lint.sh`\n\nActually re-reading the more carefully: \"Quality gates run from the project root, not with a task-directory working directory.\" and \"Include `bash /inputs/resources/team/scripts/lint.sh` in PLAN.md quality gates.\"\n\nSo I should put those four. Let me also