shes of the spec... I think including it as a single, low-stakes open question with a recommended default is the honest move and cheap for a reviewer to confirm. The spec-template explicitly says to expose uncertainty. I'll include it.\n\nAdditionally, one more genuine question: **concurrent requests** — two players clicking at nearly the same moment (or automated clients) could race. Where game state lives determines whether the server must serialize. That's covered by the same question's answer (server-held → serialize). I'll fold a sentence about concurrency into the state question or risk context.\n\nNow the \"Review\" section: keep, maybe add line noting this revision was refined against project evidence and that no prior review feedback existed. Actually the Review section describes the workflow; I'll lightly update: \"The specification Agent refined this draft from project evidence; this draft is not approval.\" Keep the gate description.\n\nAlso per template: heading \"Status\" with Version and Supersedes under it. Current structure matches. Let me restructure slightly to match template order? The template (spec-template.md) — I haven't read it. Let me read the template file to mirror the structure exactly.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"OK, so the session contains only this current attempt — the grep hits for \\\"REJECT\\\"/\\\"feedback\\\" were from the tool call serialization echoing the check.mjs source code (which contains 'VERDICT: PASS or VERDICT: REJECT'). So there is no prior review feedback available. This is a fresh spec refinement step.\\n\\nThe current SPEC.md is already quite complete and well-formed (v1 draft). My job: investigate the project (done), refine the SPEC.md per the spec-template skill, preserve human intent, record unresolved questions (as Open Questions), and ensure it passes check-spec.\\n\\nLet me now evaluate the existing draft against the spec-template checklist and the check.mjs rules:\\n\\ncheck.mjs 'spec' checks:\\n1. `/^## Acceptance criteria\\\\s*$/m` present and `AC-\\\\d+:\\\\s*\\\\S` — ✓ present in draft.\\n2. `/^## Explicit non-goals\\\\s*$/m` — ✓ present.\\n3. No `/name>|<0 \\\\/ 1 \\\\/ 2|AC-\\\\d+:\\\\s*$/` unfilled scaffold fields — need to ensure \\\"Risk tier\\\\n1 — ...\\\" doesn't match `<0 / 1 / 2`... the regex is `<name>|<0 \\\\/ 1 \\\\/ 2|AC-\\\\d+:\\\\s*$`. The draft says \\\"## Risk tier\\\\n1 — a local HTTP entry point...\\\". \\\"0 / 1 / 2\\\" as literal string — not present. Good.\\n\\nNow let me critically review the draft content:\\n\\n**Facts observed in the project:**\\n- Module `tic-tac-toe`, Go 1.21 (go.mod), toolchain go1.27.0 available.\\n- main.go: package main, board type, newBoard, render, main, playGame, parseInput, checkWin, isDraw.\\n- Existing tests: TestRender*, TestCheckWin*, TestIsDraw, TestParseInput, TestPlayGame* (wins, draw, EOF abandoned, occupied cell keeps turn, invalid input keeps turn, board unchanged on error). These cover AC-5's regression claims.\\n- ttt executable present (macOS ia32/64 binary presumably). AC-7 says don't replace it.\\n- Quality gates: build.sh → `go build -o /runtime/build/ ./...` (build to private runtime storage), lint.sh → golangci-lint v2 (check rest of lint.sh), check.mjs spec/plan/stories/verdict checks. Need to see the rest of lint.sh to know whether the plan must include it (plan check requires `/golangci|scripts\\\\/lint\\\\.sh/` in plan).\\n\\nLet me view the rest of lint.sh to see how lint runs (it printed nothing for sed -n 60,200p, so file is ~54 lines long). Let me view the whole file.\\n\\nRegarding the draft's ACs:\\n\\n- AC-1: \\\"When the documented web-mode Go command starts, the application shall serve the game UI and its assets locally without a frontend build step or remote CDN dependencies. Default web access shall be loopback-only, with a documented way to select the listen address.\\\" Good. Perhaps specify that assets are embedded or served from local files — but that's implementation detail for the plan. Keep.\\n\\n- AC-2: \\\"When a new game begins, the UI shall display an empty clickable 3-by-3 board and indicate that X moves first. When a valid cell is selected, the application shall place the current player's mark and advance the turn unless the game has ended.\\\" Good.\\n\\n- AC-3: rejection of occupied/invalid without changing board or turn; outcome display; prevent further moves until reset. Good.\\n\\n- AC-4: reset clears board/outcome, restores X; responsive narrow/mobile; understandable labels, visible feedback. Good. Note: the small-screen requirement is not verifiable via automated test easily, but it's a UI criterion; acceptable.\\n\\n- AC-5: reuse Go game-rule functions; terminal game and existing tests continue working. Good. This is the key architecture constraint from the human (\\\"no separate JS rules engine\\\" — well, the human's notes didn't explicitly say that; the draft added it as an assumption/constraint. Actually AC-5 says \\\"reuse the existing Go game-rule functions rather than introduce a separate independent JavaScript rules engine.\\\" That's a reasonable constraint preserving human intent of \\\"minimal browser interface\\\" and \\\"reusing concrete project functions\\\" per the Constraints section. It's an implementation-ish constraint but the human/previous draft already includes it; keep.)\\n\\n- AC-6: tests of new HTTP/game behavior; gates pass without weakening. Good.\\n\\n- AC-7: source placement, ttt unchanged, document run modes. Good — \\\"Document how to run the terminal and web modes\\\" — this implies a README or doc file; plan will handle that. Fine to keep in spec as it's a deliverable requirement.\\n\\nOpen questions: The draft says \\\"None currently identified.\\\" Is that honest? Let me think about material ambiguities the refinement should surface:\\n\\n1. **State management / multi-client isolation**: The assumption says two humans play one local game, no multi-game isolation needed. But with an HTTP server, a browser refresh would lose state unless the server holds it or client holds it. Since AC-5 says reuse Go game-rule functions for rules but the *board state* could live client- or server-side. Neither the human nor the draft specifies where authoritative state lives. If state lives on the server, refresh keeps the game; multiple tabs share one game; concurrency matters. If state lives in the JS client and the server only validates/executes rules, refresh resets. This is a genuine design ambiguity with observable consequences. But is it material enough to record as an open question? The draft's assumption \\\"independent accounts or multi-game isolation are not required\\\" hints a single server-side game is acceptable. I think this is worth surfacing as an open question, because \\\"prevent further moves until reset\\\" and \\\"when the user selects reset\\\" imply a single game session, but where its state resides (client vs server) affects refresh and multi-tab behavior. I'll record it briefly.\\n\\n2. **Serving assets**: embedded via Go embed vs static file server — implementation detail, not an open question for the spec. Skip.\\n\\n3. **Listen address selection**: \\\"documented way to select the listen address\\\" — flag/env var; the plan decides the exact flag name. Not an open question needing human input; the criterion already allows \\\"documented way\\\". Keep as is.\\n\\n4. **Port number**: not specified; the documented default port can be chosen by the implementer (e.g., 8080) and documented in README. Not material enough for a human question; plan records the choice. But there's a small risk default port conflicts. I'd note the default port choice as part of documentation, not an open question. Hmm — actually, a default port is something a reviewer might want to weigh in on, but it's trivial. I'll leave to plan; the AC just says loopback-only by default with documented selection.\\n\\n5. **What does \\\"malformed or invalid moves\\\" mean over HTTP?** E.g., bad cell numbers (out of range, non-numeric), occupied cells, wrong methods, bad JSON. The tests criterion AC-6 covers it; the plan will define the API contract. Could record how the API represents moves (JSON POST vs form). This is implementation detail for plan, not spec.\\n\\nSo I'll add one or two open questions: (a) where the authoritative game state lives (server or browser) and thus behavior on refresh/multiple tabs; (b) maybe default port — I'd rather fold that into plan. Actually, let me think about whether the \\\"state location\\\" question is truly open or whether the draft already decided it. The draft's Assumptions: \\\"Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts or multi-game isolation are not required.\\\" This says no isolation needed — implying one shared game for the server is fine, but it genuinely does not decide client vs server state. The observable difference: refreshing the page. If a reviewer cares, it's a one-line decision. I'll record it as an open question with a recommended default (server-held state, single game), because the spec-template says \\\"Do not silently turn guesses into requirements.\\\"\\n\\nHmm, but wait: the existing draft explicitly says under Open questions \\\"None currently identified for the stated local-game scope. If refinement finds a material ambiguity, record it for review rather than inventing additional features.\\\" So the draft itself invites recording material ambiguities. The state-location question is material enough (affects AC-2/AC-4 observability after refresh). I'll record it.\\n\\nAlso potential ambiguity: **concurrent/parallel moves** — two humans alternation implies one at a time; not needed.\\n\\nAlso: **\\\"reset\\\" semantics when game not over** — AC-4 allows reset anytime: clears board, X first. Fine.\\n\\nAlso: **is the web UI to be reachable from other devices on LAN?** Default loopback-only with documented way to select listen address. Fine.\\n\\nAnother one: **Does the server need to handle multiple independent games (multi-tab)?** The assumption says no. Fine.\\n\\nAlso I should double check AC wording for EARS patterns — they're mostly \\\"When...the system shall...\\\" which fits. Could improve AC-3 which mixes two sentences; acceptable.\\n\\nMetadata fields per the template: Status Draft, Version vN, Risk tier single 0/1/2 on the line below `## Risk tier`. Current says \\\"## Risk tier\\\\n1 — a local HTTP entry point and incremental code changes; no production deployment.\\\" The required format says \\\"A single `0`, `1`, or `2` on the line below `## Risk tier`.\\\" The current line is \\\"1 — a local HTTP entry point...\\\". Strictly, `1` is on the line, followed by an em dash explanation. The template format says a single 0/1/2 on the line below. To be safe and match the required format, I'll keep \\\"1\\\" alone on the line (moving the rationale to a following line or the risk-relevant context section, which already exists). The check.mjs doesn't validate the tier format, but the checklist says single 0,1,2 on the line below the heading. I'll put `1` alone and keep the rationale in the risk-relevant context section. Actually — risk tier rationale: risk-tiering skill: tier 1 = \\\"If a mistake could silently change routing or behavior without violating an entitlement\\\" — the general rule; Tier 0 = \\\"UI-only changes to a dashboard component itself\\\" — but this touches HTTP entry point, game-state transitions, entry point... Local HTTP entry point, no auth/credentials. Tier 1 fits (local HTTP interface, entry point). It's not Tier 0 because it introduces a network-facing entry point and state transitions. It's not Tier 2 (no credentials/identity). Keep 1.\\n\\nAlso, \\\"Last updated\\\" date — the current date 2026-10-12 is the session date. Keep as-is or update to today? The session is dated 2026-10-12; the draft was also updated that day. Keep.\\n\\nVersion: bump to v2? The draft is v1; this refinement produces a revised draft. The spec-template says \\\"Supersedes\\\" for follow-up specs, not version bumps. Hmm — version bump semantics: since this is the same task's spec review loop (attempt 1 refinement), simply refining v1 in place is fine. But a bump to v2 signals the revision clearly and the review gate ties to hashes. Previous review feedback: none. I'll bump \\\"Version: v2\\\" to reflect refinement, and note in Review section that this revision incorporates project evidence. Actually careful: \\\"agents never mark their own work approved\\\" — version bump is fine, it's not approval. And \\\"Incorporate previous review feedback\\\" — there is none; I'll note that in the Review section. I'll update the header metadata: Last updated date remains 2026-10-12 (today in this env). Version: v2.\\n\\nLet me also double check the risk-tiering recheck — the spec introduces a local HTTP interface and browser assets; no credential handling. Tier 1. Good. The go-builder skill mentions \\\"the project root containing watts.yaml\\\" — there is no watts.yaml in /workspace. Let me double-check. `ls -la /workspace` showed only go.mod, main.go, main_test.go, ttt. And `cat /workspace/watts.yaml` produced nothing. So there is no watts.yaml — the Workspace is plain. The go-builder skill says \\\"belong in the project root containing watts.yaml\\\" — that's the skill's team convention; absent here, project root = /workspace works (go.mod present). Fine, no spec impact.\\n\\nAlso double check: the plan check.mjs requires plan to include `golangci|scripts/lint\\\\.sh` — plan concern, not spec. But AC-6 already names lint gate. Fine.\\n\\nNow, do I need to check the rest of lint.sh? It's likely short (file ended before line 60). Let me confirm the whole lint.sh quickly, and also confirm no .golangci.yml exists. That's context for AC-6 wording (\\\"without weakening checks or substituting the pinned verification tools\\\") — already good.\\n\\nOne more refinement: The draft's \\\"Background and context\\\" says \\\"A macOS executable named ttt is also present\\\" — file is 2.5MB, `file ttt` would confirm. Let me run `file ttt` for accuracy in Background (it says macOS executable; confirm). Minor.\\n\\nNow let me also consider: should the spec mention how the terminal mode continues to run (`go run .` or `./ttt`)? AC-7 says \\\"Document how to run the terminal and web modes.\\\" The doc goes in README. Where should the documentation live? Spec just says document; the plan decides README.md at project root. Keep.\\n\\nAlso the AC-1 phrase \\\"the documented web-mode Go command\\\" — this implies documentation exists in the project specifying how to run web mode, creating a slight circular dependency (AC-7 documents run modes). That's fine: README will document the command; AC-1 then references it.\\n\\nPotential enhancement to AC-6: mention race detector? go-builder says use race detection for concurrent changes when toolchain supports. A web server with concurrent requests warrants `go test -race`. But spec shouldn't dictate; AC-6 says \\\"the configured build, test, vet, lint, and story-completion gates shall pass\\\". Good enough. I might add to AC-6 \\\"including concurrent/rapid requests\\\" — no, keep scope. Let me not over-engineer. Actually a web server handling concurrent clicks from two users — simultaneous move requests — is a real race scenario. But the spec says alternating at the browser; and two clicks can't truly be simultaneous from one browser. Still, HTTP servers handle requests concurrently; the plan should handle mutex/sequential state. I could add to AC-6 that tests cover concurrent or interleaved requests? Hmm, \\\"meaningful tests of new HTTP/game behavior, including malformed or invalid moves, reset, and completed-game handling\\\" — that covers the required cases; I'll leave the concurrency question to the plan and maybe mention in open questions? No — don't inflate open questions. The single-server game state on server must be goroutine-safe; that's implementer diligence, standard Go. I'll add a line in Risk-relevant context: \\\"concurrent HTTP requests share one game state; handler must be safe under concurrency.\\\" That's factual context, not a new requirement. Good.\\n\\nLet me also reconsider AC-2's \\\"unless the game has ended\\\" — after a win, further moves prevented (AC-3). Consistent.\\n\\nNow the \\\"Open questions\\\" — final list:\\n1. Where the authoritative game state lives (server-held single game vs browser-held with server-side validation), and the resulting behavior after a page refresh / in a second tab. Recommended default: server-held single game, documented — but the human should confirm since it changes UX observably. Actually, thinking about it more: does this really need a human answer? The Assumptions already say multi-game isolation not required. The natural reading: one local game. Refresh behavior is a corner; plan can decide and document. But spec-template says expose uncertainty rather than silently turning guesses into requirements. This isn't a requirement guess; it's an implementation choice. There's a risk of over-inflating. Hmm.\\n\\nLet me weigh: The draft was prepared \\\"by Codex from the user's request\\\". The user's request presumably contains something about minimal web UI. The spec explicitly says \\\"Record unresolved questions rather than inventing requirements.\\\" The state-location question is genuinely unresolved in the draft and affects observable behavior (refresh resets vs continues). I'll include it as Open Question 1 with a proposed default so a reviewer can quickly accept.\\n\\n2. Default listen address/port: AC-1 says \\\"Default web access shall be loopback-only, with a documented way to select the listen address.\\\" The default port number (e.g., 8080) is unspecified — implementer choice, documented in README. Not a material ambiguity needing human input; the criterion covers it (\\\"documented way\\\"). I'll leave it out of open questions but ensure AC-1's wording holds. Actually — wait, could I ask \\\"which port by default\\\"? That's the kind of trivial thing a reviewer won't want to answer. Skip.\\n\\n3. Whether web UI must also support the numbered-board input (type a number) or only clicking — assumption says clicking cells. Fine.\\n\\nI'll go with one open question (state location / refresh & multi-tab behavior) plus the explicit \\\"no other questions\\\" statement. Hmm, but actually, let me reconsider — is even this worth it? The spec already commits: \\\"Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts or multi-game isolation are not required.\\\" If state were browser-held, then \\\"prevent further moves until reset\\\" still holds within the page lifetime. The difference only shows after refresh. There's a plausible reading that refresh resetting to empty board is fine (matches \\\"When a new game begins\\\" = page load). But AC-1's server persists; refresh resetting the board would be \\\"starting a new game\\\" — which matches AC-2 \\\"When a new game begins, the UI shall display an empty clickable board and indicate that X moves first.\\\" So a browser-held state that resets on refresh is fully consistent with the ACs. And server-held state is also consistent. Neither violates the spec; it's purely internal. Since both work, it's arguably an implementation choice the plan resolves — not a spec ambiguity. But observable behavior differs, and the reviewer gates on hashes of the spec... I think including it as a single, low-stakes open question with a recommended default is the honest move and cheap for a reviewer to confirm. The spec-template explicitly says to expose uncertainty. I'll include it.\\n\\nAdditionally, one more genuine question: **concurrent requests** — two players clicking at nearly the same moment (or automated clients) could race. Where game state lives determines whether the server must serialize. That's covered by the same question's answer (server-held → serialize). I'll fold a sentence about concurrency into the state question or risk context.\\n\\nNow the \\\"Review\\\" section: keep, maybe add line noting this revision was refined against project evidence and that no prior review feedback existed. Actually the Review section describes the workflow; I'll lightly update: \\\"The specification Agent refined this draft from project evidence; this draft is not approval.\\\" Keep the gate description.\\n\\nAlso per template: heading \\\"Status\\\" with Version and Supersedes under it. Current structure matches. Let me restructure slightly to match template order? The template (spec-template.md) — I haven't read it. Let me read the template file to mirror the structure exactly.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_6dd13452911f461ab50bc069","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/opt/watts/kit/skills/spec-template/spec-template.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_76d578fa2bc54d388529446f","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cat /inputs/resources/team/scripts/lint.sh; echo \"=== file ttt ===\"; file /workspace/ttt; echo \"=== golangci config? ===\"; ls -la /workspace/.golangci* 2>/dev/null; find /inputs -name '.golangci*' 2>/dev/null"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":3836,"output":4408,"cacheRead":16353,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":4245,"totalTokens":24597,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791843463321,"responseId":"gen-1791843463-ojuDopJsVrGkvyEkUuoh","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},"toolResults":[{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_6dd13452911f461ab50bc069","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# SPEC: <name>\n\n## Status\nDraft / In Review / Approved / Superseded\nVersion: v1\nSupersedes: <none, or tasks/<name>/SPEC.md vN>\nOwner: <human name>\nLast updated: <date>\n\n## Intent\nWhy this exists, what problem it solves, for whom. One or two paragraphs, not a restatement of the acceptance criteria.\n\n## Background and context\nRelevant prior art, related systems, why this is being done now rather than earlier or later.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\nWrite each criterion using the EARS pattern that fits: an invariant, event, state, unwanted condition, or optional feature followed by the system response. Combine a state and event only when both matter. Not \"handles errors gracefully,\" that's not testable. Number each one (AC-1, AC-2, ...) so PLAN.md's user stories can name which criteria they cover. Example:\n\n- AC-1: While the queue is non-empty, when a worker polls, the dispatcher shall return exactly one unclaimed task.\n- AC-2: While no tasks are available, when a worker polls, the dispatcher shall return an empty result within 200ms, not block.\n\n- AC-3:\n\n## Explicit non-goals\nWhat this deliberately does not do. Required, not optional, this is what the admission rubric checks a diff against for invented scope.\n\n-\n\n## Assumptions\nWhat's being taken as given. Worth stating explicitly, because if one of these is wrong, the spec is wrong, not the implementation.\n\n-\n\n## Constraints\nTechnical, regulatory, timeline, or resourcing limits that shape the acceptance criteria above.\n\n-\n\n## Open questions\nAmbiguity that has to be resolved by a human before PLAN.md gets written, not silently assumed by whoever implements this. If this section is empty, that's worth a second look, not a sign everything's clear.\n\n-\n\n## Risk-relevant context\n- Touches:\n- Does not touch:\n\n## Risk tier\n<0 / 1 / 2, from the risk-tiering skill>\n\n## Review\n\nAn agent drafts this document. The human approves or rejects it at the spec-review workflow gate with `wctl task decide <task> spec-review`, optionally using `--reject --feedback 'changes needed'`.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791843498029},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_76d578fa2bc54d388529446f","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n\n# Watts runs this container command from the isolated application workspace.\nif [[ ! -f go.mod ]]; then\n  echo 'lint: run from the Go project root containing go.mod' >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nif ! command -v golangci-lint >/dev/null 2>&1; then\n  echo 'lint: golangci-lint v2 must be installed in the workload image' >&2\n  exit 1\nfi\n\nscript_dir=\"$(cd -- \"$(dirname -- \"${BASH_SOURCE[0]}\")\" && pwd)\"\nexec golangci-lint run --config \"$script_dir/../.golangci.yml\" ./...\n=== file ttt ===\n/bin/bash: line 1: file: command not found\n=== golangci config? ===\n/inputs/resources/team/.golangci.yml\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791843498030}]}
