, `1`, or `2` on the line below `## Risk tier`.\" I'll comply: `## Risk tier\\n1` and move rationale into risk-relevant context. Actually the current draft has \"1 — a local HTTP entry point and incremental code changes; no production deployment.\" To be safe with the format and the check (which doesn't check this), I'll put just \"1\" and add a bullet in Risk-relevant context explaining the tier. That's cleaner.\n\nHmm, actually let me re-read: the spec-template skill's Save-and-review table says: \"Risk tier | A single 0, 1, or 2 on the line below `## Risk tier`.\" Yes — put just the digit. I'll do that and fold the rationale into \"Risk-relevant context\" bullets.\n\n7. Assumptions: keep and possibly sharpen with the observed tools (Go stdlib HTTP server). Keep the existing three, maybe add one about go.mod module name/Go version? Not necessary. Keep as-is; maybe slightly reword the third (workflow agents refinement) — the third is a workflow note; it's fine to keep but it's not an assumption about the system; it's fine as-is. Actually, I might fold that into Review section instead. Keep Assumptions focused: (1) single local game, no isolation, (2) stdlib server + static HTML/CSS/JS, minimal deps, (3) human instruction says... hmm the current assumption 3: \"The workflow's specification and planning Agents will refine these notes and create the implementation plan; this draft is not approval.\" That's a workflow statement, not an assumption about the domain. Move it to Review section. I'll replace with: \"The existing ttt binary and go.mod (Go 1.21) remain the project baseline; web additions compile within the same module.\" That's more of a constraint. Hmm, keep assumptions meaningful:\n\n- Two humans play one local game at a time; no accounts, isolation, or multi-game support is required.\n- A standard-library Go HTTP server serving local HTML/CSS/JavaScript is the intended approach; no framework, package manager, or build pipeline.\n- Browser capabilities: assume a modern browser supporting ES6+ and CSS grid/flexbox; no legacy browser support is required. — is this inventing? It's a reasonable assumption supporting AC-4. I'll include briefly.\n\n8. Constraints: keep existing four, maybe sharpen first (already good). Add: \"The module remains `tic-tac-toe` at go 1.21; no dependency additions are required and new dependencies should be avoided.\" That duplicates \"Keep dependencies minimal\" assumption — I can fold into Constraints as \"no new third-party dependencies.\" I'd rather keep constraints concise; fold \"no new dependencies\" into the stdlib assumption/constraint.\n\n9. Review section: replace workflow-only text with template-like: agent drafts, human approves/rejects at spec-review gate; note this revision responds to project evidence and there was no prior rejection feedback in this environment. Also keep a note that the previous draft's Assumption 3 moved here. Keep it short.\n\n10. Also mention in Background the build products location: build.sh outputs to /runtime/build (private storage) — AC-7 references \"Verification build products/caches shall remain under private runtime storage\" — confirmed by build.sh. Good, cite it.\n\nAlso confirm \"supersedes: none\" stays.\n\nOne more check — the check.mjs spec gate requires `AC-\\d+:\\s*\\S` and headings; our doc passes. Also ensure no literal \"0 / 1 / 2\" string remains — my Risk tier line will be just \"1\".\n\nWord choice for Open Questions:\n\n**OQ-1: Where does the authoritative game state live, and what happens after a page refresh or when two tabs are open?** The draft assumes one local game with no isolation, but does not commit whether the Go server or the browser page holds the board. This is observable: with server-held state, a refresh continues the same game; with page-held state, a refresh starts a new game (consistent with AC-2). Recommended default: server holds a single game's state; refreshing the page returns to the current board, and concurrent requests are serialized. Otherwise the plan must specify one and document it.\n\nShould I also ask about default port? The draft AC-1 says documented way to select listen address; the concrete default port (e.g., 127.0.0.1:8080) is plan-level documentation choice. I'll not raise it; add a sentence to AC-1? No — keep AC-1. The README documents it. Fine.\n\nLet me also make sure the ACs keep \"system actor\" concrete: \"the application shall...\" or \"the UI shall...\". AC-2 says \"the UI shall display...\" then \"the application shall place the current player's mark and advance the turn\" — mixing actors UI/application is OK-ish; I can unify: \"the application shall display an empty clickable 3-by-3 board\" hmm. Typically the server application renders the board via served assets. I'll use \"the web UI shall display\" and \"the application shall place...\". Slightly cleaner: split AC-2 into display (state: new game) and move handling (event: valid cell selected). Already does that within one criterion using when + conjunction. Fine, but I can improve to an explicit EARS two-part. Let me keep the criterion but polish:\n\n- AC-2: When a new game begins, the web UI shall display an empty clickable 3-by-3 board and show that X moves first. When the user selects a valid empty cell, the application shall place the current player's mark and, unless the game has ended, advance the turn to the other player.\n\nThat's good.\n\nAC-3: \"If the user selects an occupied or invalid cell, then the application shall reject the move without changing the board or the current player's turn. When either player completes a winning line or the board fills without a winner, the UI shall display the outcome (winner or draw) and reject further moves until the user selects reset.\"\n\nAC-4: \"When the user selects reset, the application shall clear the board, remove the previous outcome, and restore X as the current player. The UI shall remain usable on narrow/mobile viewports and shall provide readable button labels and visible turn and outcome feedback.\"\n\nAC-6: \"The change shall include tests of the new HTTP and game behavior covering valid moves and turn advancement, occupied and malformed cells, unknown requests or bad payloads, reset, and games that end in a win or draw; after a game ends, further moves shall be refused. The configured build, test, vet, lint, and story-completion gates shall pass without weakening checks or substituting the pinned verification tools.\"\n\nHmm wait — \"unknown requests or bad payloads\" — is that over-specifying? AC-6 already says \"malformed or invalid moves\". I'll keep it close to the original to preserve intent but add the \"after a game ends, further moves refused\" test point, which AC-3's behavior implies should be tested. The draft AC-6: \"including malformed or invalid moves, reset, and completed-game handling.\" Fine — I can keep mostly and add named cases: malformed payload, occupied cell, out-of-range cell, reset, and moves attempted after win/draw. Good.\n\nAC-7 keep as-is (maybe split run-docs into its own sentence — it already is).\n\nAlso AC-1: keep. Maybe add \"with no frontend build step or remote CDN dependencies\" — keep as-is.\n\nNow Background and context — add observed facts:\n\n\"The project root /workspace contains go.mod (module tic-tac-toe, Go 1.21), main.go, main_test.go, and the ttt executable. main.go implements board (a 3×3 rune grid), newBoard, render, playGame (terminal loop), parseInput, checkWin (all eight lines), and isDraw; main() runs the terminal game over stdin/stdout. main_test.go covers render output, all eight winning lines for both players, draw detection, parse input boundaries, X/O wins, draw, EOF abandonment, occupied-cell and invalid-input turn retention, and board-unchanged-on-error. The team gates (build.sh, lint.sh, check.mjs under /inputs/resources/team/scripts) build to /runtime/build, run golangci-lint with the team config, and validate spec/plan/stories/verdict artifacts; ttt must remain untouched.\"\n\nAlso mention that `file` isn't available but binary present (macOS executable per existing context — the draft says so; I verified size only. Keep the draft's claim; it came from prior agent knowledge. Actually I shouldn't claim \"macOS\" as new fact without verifying; the draft already states it, presumably from the previous agent's inspection. I can keep it attributed as observed in the existing notes. Or phrase \"an existing executable named ttt\" without OS claim. Hmm — the human draft says \"A macOS executable named ttt is also present\". Keep the human's phrasing. Fine.\n\nRisk-relevant context additions:\n- Touches: local HTTP listener and request handling, browser assets served from the module, application entry point (web-mode command), game-state transitions shared with terminal mode, new HTTP tests, run documentation.\n- Does not touch: authentication, credentials, production services, databases, deployment, external accounts, historical task evidence, the ttt binary.\n- Note: requests may arrive concurrently while one game state is shared; the implementation must be safe under concurrency (race-free). This informs AC-6 testing. — put as its own bullet under Risk-relevant context or in Assumptions/Constraints? I'll add to Risk-relevant context.\n\nTier rationale: Tier 1: mistake could silently change behavior (game state via a network-facing entry point on the dev machine) without violating entitlements or touching credentials; no production deployment; no identity/entitlement logic. It's clearly not Tier 0 because it adds a network-visible entry point and state transitions; not Tier 2 because no credentials/identity.\n\nNow the Review section: \n\n\"An agent drafts this document; the workflow pauses at the spec-review gate for a human decision (or an explicitly delegated coordinator decision) tied to the reviewed artifact hashes. This is draft v2: it refines the v1 draft against project evidence (main.go,