g, and validate spec/plan/stories/verdict artifacts; ttt must remain untouched.\\\"\\n\\nAlso mention that `file` isn't available but binary present (macOS executable per existing context — the draft says so; I verified size only. Keep the draft's claim; it came from prior agent knowledge. Actually I shouldn't claim \\\"macOS\\\" as new fact without verifying; the draft already states it, presumably from the previous agent's inspection. I can keep it attributed as observed in the existing notes. Or phrase \\\"an existing executable named ttt\\\" without OS claim. Hmm — the human draft says \\\"A macOS executable named ttt is also present\\\". Keep the human's phrasing. Fine.\\n\\nRisk-relevant context additions:\\n- Touches: local HTTP listener and request handling, browser assets served from the module, application entry point (web-mode command), game-state transitions shared with terminal mode, new HTTP tests, run documentation.\\n- Does not touch: authentication, credentials, production services, databases, deployment, external accounts, historical task evidence, the ttt binary.\\n- Note: requests may arrive concurrently while one game state is shared; the implementation must be safe under concurrency (race-free). This informs AC-6 testing. — put as its own bullet under Risk-relevant context or in Assumptions/Constraints? I'll add to Risk-relevant context.\\n\\nTier rationale: Tier 1: mistake could silently change behavior (game state via a network-facing entry point on the dev machine) without violating entitlements or touching credentials; no production deployment; no identity/entitlement logic. It's clearly not Tier 0 because it adds a network-visible entry point and state transitions; not Tier 2 because no credentials/identity.\\n\\nNow the Review section: \\n\\n\\\"An agent drafts this document; the workflow pauses at the spec-review gate for a human decision (or an explicitly delegated coordinator decision) tied to the reviewed artifact hashes. This is draft v2: it refines the v1 draft against project evidence (main.go, main_test.go, go.mod, team gate scripts). No prior rejection feedback was available in this environment to incorporate; if the reviewer has feedback, this draft will be revised in place using that feedback rather than superseded. A signature in this document does not approve the workflow.\\\"\\n\\nGood. Also, keep Owner line.\\n\\nOne more consideration — the task instructions say \\\"Application source, tests, and web assets shall live in the project root\\\" — that's AC-7 already. Also \\\"The task directory holds workflow documents... only; do not create application code inside it\\\" — AC-7 partially. Add explicit line in Constraints: \\\"Do not create a new application or module under the task directory\\\" (already present). Good.\\n\\nAlso the human prompt says \\\"the package descriptor may live elsewhere\\\" — go.mod is in /workspace. Fine.\\n\\nLet me also double-check: is there a \\\"README.md\\\" in the workspace? No — only go.mod, main.go, main_test.go, ttt. AC-7 requires documenting how to run terminal and web modes — implies creating a doc (README.md) at project root. The spec should perhaps name where documentation goes? \\\"Document how to run the terminal and web modes.\\\" I'll keep it general: documentation in the project root (README.md) — hmm, naming the file is plan-ish. Keep general but note \\\"in the project root.\\\" Actually to make it verifiable, say: \\\"The change shall document the web-mode command and the terminal-mode command in the project root (for example, a README).\\\" Slight plan intrusion; acceptable to add \\\"(for example, a README.md)\\\"? The human draft says just \\\"Document how to run the terminal and web modes.\\\" I'll keep the human wording and in the plan the agent will choose README.md. Keep as-is.\\n\\nAlright — also fix the AC-6 in the draft which says \\\"The configured build, test, vet, lint, and story-completion gates shall pass without weakening checks or substituting the pinned verification tools.\\\" Keep verbatim (it references team gates; verified: build.sh/lint.sh/check.mjs exist).\\n\\nNote: there's no `vet` script, but \\\"vet\\\" presumably = `go vet` run by the builder. Keep as-is (human text).\\n\\nNow write the file. Let me compose the final SPEC.md.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"No prior review feedback exists in this environment (fresh spec step, no `review/` artifacts); the current SPEC.md is a v1 draft prepared from the human's request that I'll refine against project evidence. Let me check the team lint config briefly, then write the updated spec."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_352c171d1dee492c9387a03e","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"sed -n 1,60p /inputs/resources/team/.golangci.yml"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":3288,"output":3221,"cacheRead":22003,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":3102,"totalTokens":28512,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791843498035,"responseId":"gen-1791843498-VK8yrXbE9aKQmccJmfsM","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_352c171d1dee492c9387a03e","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"version: \"2\"\n\nrun:\n  timeout: 5m\n  relative-path-mode: gomod\n  tests: true\n\nlinters:\n  default: none\n  enable:\n    - errcheck\n    - govet\n    - ineffassign\n    - staticcheck\n    - unused\n\nformatters:\n  enable:\n    - gofmt\n\nissues:\n  max-issues-per-linter: 0\n  max-same-issues: 0\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791843524346},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed: team lint config (errcheck, govet, ineffassign, staticcheck, unused + gofmt). Now write the refined SPEC.md.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"Confirmed: team lint config (errcheck, govet, ineffassign, staticcheck, unused + gofmt). Now write the refined SPEC.md.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Now I'll write the refined SPEC.md, preserving all human intent and constraints while grounding the draft in the observed project evidence:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_8d08262ab84d4f0ebabae947","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/runtime/task/SPEC.md","content":"# SPEC: Tic-tac-toe web UI\n\n## Status\nDraft\nVersion: v2\nSupersedes: none; this is the same task's draft refined at the spec step (v1 was the initial draft from the user's request)\nOwner: project user; draft refined by the specification agent\nLast updated: 2026-10-12\n\n## Intent\nAdd a minimal browser interface to the existing Go tic-tac-toe game so two people can play locally by clicking cells. This is an incremental change to the current application, not a replacement project. Preserve the terminal game while making the same rules available through a web UI.\n\n## Background and context\nThe project root (`/workspace`) already contains `go.mod` (module `tic-tac-toe`, Go 1.21), `main.go`, `main_test.go`, and an existing executable named `ttt`. Observed current behavior in `main.go`:\n\n- `board` is a 3×3 rune grid; `newBoard` starts all cells empty.\n- `playGame` runs the terminal loop over stdin/stdout with a numbered board, alternating X/O turns, and prompts after each move.\n- `parseInput` converts a single digit 1–9 to a position (0–8) and rejects empty, multi-character, and out-of-range input.\n- `checkWin` evaluates all eight winning lines (3 rows, 3 columns, 2 diagonals); `isDraw` detects a full board with no winner.\n- `main` invokes the terminal game and exits 0 on win, draw, or EOF (abandoned game).\n\n`main_test.go` establishes regression coverage for numbered rendering, all eight winning lines for both players, draw detection, input parsing boundaries, X/O wins, draws, EOF behavior, occupied-cell and invalid-input turn retention, and boards staying unchanged on rejected moves.\n\nThe team quality gates under `/inputs/resources/team/scripts/` build to private runtime storage (`build.sh` writes to `/runtime/build`), run `golangci-lint` with the pinned team config (`lint.sh`, config at `/inputs/resources/team/.golangci.yml`), and validate the spec/plan/story/verdict artifacts (`check.mjs`). The `ttt` executable must remain untouched; this task must not replace it with a container-built binary.\n\nThis task adds a loopback HTTP server and browser assets in the same module so the same game rules are reachable from a browser without changing the terminal experience.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- AC-1: When the documented web-mode Go command starts, the application shall serve the game UI and its assets locally without a frontend build step or remote CDN dependencies. Default web access shall be loopback-only, with a documented way to select the listen address.\n- AC-2: When a new game begins, the web UI shall display an empty clickable 3-by-3 board and show that X moves first. When the user selects a valid empty cell, the application shall place the current player's mark and, unless the game has ended, advance the turn to the other player.\n- AC-3: If the user selects an occupied or invalid cell, then the application shall reject the move without changing the board or the current player's turn. When either player completes a winning line or the board fills without a winner, the UI shall display the correct outcome and reject further moves until the user selects reset.\n- AC-4: When the user selects reset, the application shall clear the board, remove the previous outcome, and restore X as the current player. The UI shall remain usable on narrow/mobile viewports and shall provide readable button labels and visible turn and outcome feedback.\n- AC-5: The web interface shall reuse the existing Go game-rule functions rather than introduce a separate independent JavaScript rules engine. The terminal game and its existing tests shall continue to work, including invalid moves retaining turns, wins/draws, and EOF behavior.\n- AC-6: The change shall include tests of the new HTTP and game behavior covering valid moves and turn advancement, occupied cells, malformed or out-of-range cells, bad request payloads, reset, and completed-game handling, including moves attempted after a win or draw. The configured build, test, vet, lint, and story-completion gates shall pass without weakening checks or substituting the pinned verification tools.\n- AC-7: Application source, tests, and web assets shall live in the project root and normal source directories. Task documents and evidence shall remain in the task directory. Verification build products/caches shall remain under private runtime storage; the existing `ttt` executable shall remain unchanged. The change shall document how to run the terminal and web modes.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n- No AI opponent, remote multiplayer, authentication, database, persistence across server restarts, deployment, or framework migration.\n- No frontend framework or JavaScript package manager/build pipeline is needed.\n- No changes to previous Watts task/eval records or runtime-owned metadata to force success.\n- No new third-party dependencies; the implementation should need only the Go standard library.\n\n## Assumptions\n- Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts, multi-game isolation, and simultaneous games in multiple tabs are not required.\n- A small standard-library Go HTTP server and local HTML/CSS/JavaScript are an appropriate implementation. Keep dependencies minimal.\n- The UI targets a modern browser with standard ES6+ and CSS layout support; no legacy-browser support is required.\n\n## Constraints\n- Preserve existing game behavior and prefer reusing concrete project functions over introducing a generic game framework.\n- Do not create a new application or module under the task directory.\n- Use the image's declared tools and quality gates. Do not install replacements or change team lint policy to suppress findings.\n- Do not read, expose, or embed provider credentials in application files or served responses.\n\n## Open questions\n- OQ-1: Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab? The v1 draft assumes one local game with no isolation but does not commit to whether the Go server or the browser page holds the board. This is observable: with server-held state a refresh resumes the same game, while with page-held state a refresh starts a new game (both are consistent with the criteria above). Recommended default: the server holds a single game's state, a refresh returns the current board, and concurrent requests are serialized. If a reviewer prefers page-held state, that should be confirmed here rather than silently chosen by the implementer.\n- No other material ambiguities were identified for the stated local-game scope. If review surfaces further ambiguity, it will be recorded here rather than turned into a requirement.\n\n## Risk-relevant context\n- Touches: local HTTP listener and request handling, browser assets served from the module, application entry point for web mode, game-state transitions shared with the terminal mode, new HTTP tests, and run documentation.\n- Does not touch: authentication, credential handling, production services, databases, deployment, external accounts, historical task evidence, or the existing `ttt` binary.\n- Requests may arrive concurrently while one game state is shared; the web handler must be race-free and must serialize state changes against the terminal game's rule functions.\n\n## Risk tier\n1\n\nRationale: the change adds a network-visible entry point and incremental game-state/logic changes on a developer machine; a mistake could silently change behavior but cannot touch identities, credentials, or entitlements, and there is no production deployment.\n\n## Review\n\nAn agent drafts this document. The declared spec-review Procedure then requires a human decision — or an explicitly delegated coordinator decision — tied to the reviewed artifact hashes. This v2 draft refines v1 against project evidence (`main.go`, `main_test.go`, `go.mod`, and the team gate scripts); no prior rejection feedback was available in this environment to incorporate. On rejection, this draft is revised in place using the feedback while preserving the original intent. A signature in this document does not approve the workflow."}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":3149,"output":1796,"cacheRead":25471,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":29,"totalTokens":30416,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791843524352,"responseId":"gen-1791843524-H5tHT7TNaRbMpo6Ayn0A","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_8d08262ab84d4f0ebabae947","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote to /runtime/task/SPEC.md"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791843537431},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now validate the spec artifact with the team's pinned gate:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_43182955a4c74150a4ff0c41","name":"check-spec","arguments":{}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":4262,"output":41,"cacheRead":26175,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":30478,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791843537434,"responseId":"gen-1791843537-lDRaecveDvLNzcKlJDPo","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_43182955a4c74150a4ff0c41","toolName":"check-spec","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"tool\":\"check-spec\",\"argv\":[\"node\",\"/inputs/resources/team/scripts/check.mjs\",\"spec\",\"/runtime/task\"],\"working_dir\":\"/runtime/task\",\"status\":\"exited\",\"exit_code\":0,\"stdout\":\"spec: OK\\n\",\"stderr\":\"\",\"truncated\":false,\"evidence\":\"tools/da241e0d-c505-4ee8-a582-dbe17669fe50.json\",\"duration_ms\":44}"}],"details":{"tool":"check-spec","argv":["node","/inputs/resources/team/scripts/check.mjs","spec","/runtime/task"],"working_dir":"/runtime/task","status":"exited","exit_code":0,"stdout":"spec: OK\n","stderr":"","truncated":false,"evidence":"tools/da241e0d-c505-4ee8-a582-dbe17669fe50.json","duration_ms":44},"isError":false,"timestamp":1791843540318},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The spec gate passes. Let me do a final review of the written file to make sure everything is intact.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"The spec gate passes. Let me do a final review of the written file to make sure everything is intact.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"The `spec` gate passes. Final review of the written artifact:"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_124a26138a144dbeacd6e59a","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/runtime/task/SPEC.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":4588,"output":85,"cacheRead":25998,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":20,"totalTokens":30671,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1791843540321,"responseId":"gen-1791843540-4Z5NPZ2YfYB3r9WTgCez","rawStopReason":"tool_calls","thinkingLevel":"low"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_124a26138a144dbeacd6e59a","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# SPEC: Tic-tac-toe web UI\n\n## Status\nDraft\nVersion: v2\nSupersedes: none; this is the same task's draft refined at the spec step (v1 was the initial draft from the user's request)\nOwner: project user; draft refined by the specification agent\nLast updated: 2026-10-12\n\n## Intent\nAdd a minimal browser interface to the existing Go tic-tac-toe game so two people can play locally by clicking cells. This is an incremental change to the current application, not a replacement project. Preserve the terminal game while making the same rules available through a web UI.\n\n## Background and context\nThe project root (`/workspace`) already contains `go.mod` (module `tic-tac-toe`, Go 1.21), `main.go`, `main_test.go`, and an existing executable named `ttt`. Observed current behavior in `main.go`:\n\n- `board` is a 3×3 rune grid; `newBoard` starts all cells empty.\n- `playGame` runs the terminal loop over stdin/stdout with a numbered board, alternating X/O turns, and prompts after each move.\n- `parseInput` converts a single digit 1–9 to a position (0–8) and rejects empty, multi-character, and out-of-range input.\n- `checkWin` evaluates all eight winning lines (3 rows, 3 columns, 2 diagonals); `isDraw` detects a full board with no winner.\n- `main` invokes the terminal game and exits 0 on win, draw, or EOF (abandoned game).\n\n`main_test.go` establishes regression coverage for numbered rendering, all eight winning lines for both players, draw detection, input parsing boundaries, X/O wins, draws, EOF behavior, occupied-cell and invalid-input turn retention, and boards staying unchanged on rejected moves.\n\nThe team quality gates under `/inputs/resources/team/scripts/` build to private runtime storage (`build.sh` writes to `/runtime/build`), run `golangci-lint` with the pinned team config (`lint.sh`, config at `/inputs/resources/team/.golangci.yml`), and validate the spec/plan/story/verdict artifacts (`check.mjs`). The `ttt` executable must remain untouched; this task must not replace it with a container-built binary.\n\nThis task adds a loopback HTTP server and browser assets in the same module so the same game rules are reachable from a browser without changing the terminal experience.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- AC-1: When the documented web-mode Go command starts, the application shall serve the game UI and its assets locally without a frontend build step or remote CDN dependencies. Default web access shall be loopback-only, with a documented way to select the listen address.\n- AC-2: When a new game begins, the web UI shall display an empty clickable 3-by-3 board and show that X moves first. When the user selects a valid empty cell, the application shall place the current player's mark and, unless the game has ended, advance the turn to the other player.\n- AC-3: If the user selects an occupied or invalid cell, then the application shall reject the move without changing the board or the current player's turn. When either player completes a winning line or the board fills without a winner, the UI shall display the correct outcome and reject further moves until the user selects reset.\n- AC-4: When the user selects reset, the application shall clear the board, remove the previous outcome, and restore X as the current player. The UI shall remain usable on narrow/mobile viewports and shall provide readable button labels and visible turn and outcome feedback.\n- AC-5: The web interface shall reuse the existing Go game-rule functions rather than introduce a separate independent JavaScript rules engine. The terminal game and its existing tests shall continue to work, including invalid moves retaining turns, wins/draws, and EOF behavior.\n- AC-6: The change shall include tests of the new HTTP and game behavior covering valid moves and turn advancement, occupied cells, malformed or out-of-range cells, bad request payloads, reset, and completed-game handling, including moves attempted after a win or draw. The configured build, test, vet, lint, and story-completion gates shall pass without weakening checks or substituting the pinned verification tools.\n- AC-7: Application source, tests, and web assets shall live in the project root and normal source directories. Task documents and evidence shall remain in the task directory. Verification build products/caches shall remain under private runtime storage; the existing `ttt` executable shall remain unchanged. The change shall document how to run the terminal and web modes.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n- No AI opponent, remote multiplayer, authentication, database, persistence across server restarts, deployment, or framework migration.\n- No frontend framework or JavaScript package manager/build pipeline is needed.\n- No changes to previous Watts task/eval records or runtime-owned metadata to force success.\n- No new third-party dependencies; the implementation should need only the Go standard library.\n\n## Assumptions\n- Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts, multi-game isolation, and simultaneous games in multiple tabs are not required.\n- A small standard-library Go HTTP server and local HTML/CSS/JavaScript are an appropriate implementation. Keep dependencies minimal.\n- The UI targets a modern browser with standard ES6+ and CSS layout support; no legacy-browser support is required.\n\n## Constraints\n- Preserve existing game behavior and prefer reusing concrete project functions over introducing a generic game framework.\n- Do not create a new application or module under the task directory.\n- Use the image's declared tools and quality gates. Do not install replacements or change team lint policy to suppress findings.\n- Do not read, expose, or embed provider credentials in application files or served responses.\n\n## Open questions\n- OQ-1: Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab? The v1 draft assumes one local game with no isolation but does not commit to whether the Go server or the browser page holds the board. This is observable: with server-held state a refresh resumes the same game, while with page-held state a refresh starts a new game (both are consistent with the criteria above). Recommended default: the server holds a single game's state, a refresh returns the current board, and concurrent requests are serialized. If a reviewer prefers page-held state, that should be confirmed here rather than silently chosen by the implementer.\n- No other material ambiguities were identified for the stated local-game scope. If review surfaces further ambiguity, it will be recorded here rather than turned into a requirement.\n\n## Risk-relevant context\n- Touches: local HTTP listener and request handling, browser assets served from the module, application entry point for web mode, game-state transitions shared with the terminal mode, new HTTP tests, and run documentation.\n- Does not touch: authentication, credential handling, production services, databases, deployment, external accounts, historical task evidence, or the existing `ttt` binary.\n- Requests may arrive concurrently while one game state is shared; the web handler must be race-free and must serialize state changes against the terminal game's rule functions.\n\n## Risk tier\n1\n\nRationale: the change adds a network-visible entry point and incremental game-state/logic changes on a developer machine; a mistake could silently change behavior but cannot touch identities, credentials, or entitlements, and there is no production deployment.\n\n## Review\n\nAn agent drafts this document. The declared spec-review Procedure then requires a human decision — or an explicitly delegated coordinator decision — tied to the reviewed artifact hashes. This v2 draft refines v1 against project evidence (`main.go`, `main_test.go`, `go.mod`, and the team gate scripts); no prior rejection feedback was available in this environment to incorporate. On rejection, this draft is revised in place using the feedback while preserving the original intent. A signature in this document does not approve the workflow."}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1791843543186},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The SPEC.md is complete and passes the spec gate. Let me summarize what I did.","thinkingSignature":"[{\"type\":\"reasoning.text\",\"text\":\"The SPEC.md is complete and passes the spec gate. Let me summarize what I did.\",\"format\":\"unknown\",\"index\":0}]"},{"type":"text","text":"Done. I refined `/runtime/task/SPEC.md` per the spec-template skill and it passes the pinned `spec` gate (`spec: OK`).\n\n**What I did**\n\n1. **Preserved the human request** — Kept all v1 intent, acceptance criteria, non-goals, assumptions, constraints, and the \"`ttt` must remain untouched\" requirement intact; no blank-template overwrite.\n\n2. **Investigated the project** — Verified:\n   - `go.mod` (module `tic-tac-toe`, Go 1.21), `main.go` (`board`/`newBoard`/`render`/`playGame`/`parseInput`/`checkWin`/`isDraw`), `main_test.go` (wins, draws, EOF, invalid-input turn retention, board-unchanged rejection), existing `ttt` binary.\n   - Team gates: `build.sh` (builds to `/runtime/build`), `lint.sh` (pinned `golangci-lint` + team config, no suppressions), `check.mjs` (spec/plan/story/verdict checks).\n\n3. **Refined the draft** — Bumped to v2 and grounded the Background with observed facts and cited paths; clarified AC-3 (unwanted-condition pattern) and AC-6 (concrete test cases including post-game moves); aligned the Risk tier line to the template format (`1` alone, rationale moved below); noted concurrency in the risk context.\n\n4. **Recorded one genuine ambiguity, didn't invent requirements** — OQ-1: whether the server or the page holds authoritative game state (observable via refresh/multi-tab), with a recommended default for the reviewer. No prior rejection feedback existed in this environment (no `review/` artifacts, fresh session), so none could be incorporated.\n\nNo code was written and nothing was approved — the workflow still pauses at `spec-review` for a human decision against the artifact hash."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"openrouter","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-flash","usage":{"input":5825,"output":437,