{"type":"tool_execution_end","toolCallId":"call_a9633c1cb2c3409cbc2661a9","toolName":"read","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: spec-template\ndescription: Draft or refine a task's SPEC.md from human intent, project evidence, and review feedback before implementation planning.\n---\n\n# Draft a software specification\n\nTurn the human's request into a clear, reviewable `SPEC.md`. A rough request is enough to begin. The human supplies intent; the agent drafts the specification.\n\n## Preserve the request\n\n- Read the existing task `SPEC.md` before editing it.\n- Preserve human intent, constraints, non-goals, and substantive content when reorganizing the draft.\n- Use [spec-template.md](spec-template.md) as a structure reference. Copy it only if `SPEC.md` does not exist; never replace an existing draft with a blank template.\n- If the file contains only boilerplate, record the missing intent under Open Questions and ask the human for their request. Do not invent a task.\n- Write only in the selected task directory, never at the project root or in another task.\n\n## Drafting checklist\n\n1. **Establish the purpose.** Identify the affected user, their problem, and the observable result they need. Preserve the meaning of the human's request.\n2. **Inspect the project.** Read relevant code and documentation. Describe current behavior and cite relevant project paths. Separate observed facts, assumptions, and unanswered questions.\n3. **Bound the scope.** State what the change includes and excludes. Carry forward supplied constraints. Do not add adjacent features simply because they seem useful.\n4. **Specify behavior.** Write numbered acceptance criteria for the requested behavior, including relevant failure cases and boundary conditions. Every criterion must be verifiable.\n5. **Expose uncertainty.** Put unresolved decisions under Open Questions, explaining what needs an answer. Do not silently turn guesses into requirements or invent thresholds.\n6. **Assess risk.** Use the `risk-tiering` skill to assign the tier from the actual affected behavior.\n7. **Edit for clarity.** Apply the writing checklist below, then save the completed draft to `SPEC.md`.\n\n`SPEC.md` explains what the system must do and why. Implementation approach, code structure, story sequencing, and test execution belong in `PLAN.md`. Keep human-mandated technical constraints in the spec, but do not invent an implementation there.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\nUse `- AC-1: ...`, `- AC-2: ...`, and so on. Give each criterion one observable response and a concrete system actor. Choose the EARS pattern that fits the behavior:\n\n| Behavior | Pattern |\n| --- | --- |\n| Always required | The system shall perform the response. |\n| Triggered by an event | When the event occurs, the system shall perform the response. |\n| Applies during a state | While the state holds, the system shall perform the response. |\n| Handles unwanted behavior | If the unwanted condition occurs, then the system shall perform the response. |\n| Depends on an optional feature | Where the feature is enabled, the system shall perform the response. |\n\nCombine a state and event when both matter. Do not force every criterion into the same pattern.\n\nFor example:\n\n- AC-1: When the user selects a task, the monitor shall display that task's workflow stages.\n- AC-2: If the execution service cannot answer a status query, then the monitor shall identify the task's execution status as unavailable.\n\nReplace vague claims such as “fast,” “robust,” or “user-friendly” with observable behavior or a human-supplied measurable target. If a target is missing, record the question. The plan must map its stories to these criterion IDs.\n\n## Writing checklist\n\n- Lead with the purpose and write for the human who will approve the work.\n- Use consistent names for users, components, and actions. Define unfamiliar terms when first used.\n- Prefer active voice with an explicit actor. Replace ambiguous pronouns with the component's name.\n- Keep sentences short and paragraphs focused on one topic.\n- Use headings to separate concerns, lists for parallel items, and tables for comparisons.\n- Remove repeated statements, template instructions, and unfilled placeholders from the draft.\n- Keep facts, assumptions, constraints, and questions distinguishable.\n\n## Save and review\n\nUse the bundled template's headings and metadata format:\n\n| Field | Required format |\n| --- | --- |\n| Status | `Draft` or `In Review` on the line below `## Status`; agents never mark their own work approved. |\n| Version | `Version: vN` in the Status section. |\n| Risk tier | A single `0`, `1`, or `2` on the line below `## Risk tier`. |\n| Acceptance criteria | Numbered `- AC-N: ...` entries with substantive content. |\n| Explicit non-goals | Concrete scope exclusions rather than template filler. |\n| Open questions | Actual unresolved decisions, or an explicit statement that none remain. |\n\nThe default workflow pauses at `spec-review`. Human approval is recorded by the workflow gate against the document hash; a signature in the document does not approve the workflow. Never sign for a human or approve your own output.\n\nOn rejection, revise the existing draft using the feedback while preserving the original intent. Follow-up work on a completed task belongs in a new task folder; reference the preceding specification in `Supersedes`. Do not create a new task merely to revise a draft during its review loop.\n\n## Sources\n\nThis checklist condenses the following guidance into Watts' existing spec and plan workflow. It does not require installing their tools.\n\n- [GitHub Spec Kit](https://github.com/github/spec-kit), MIT: separate what and why from technical planning.\n- [Microsoft EARS acceptance guidance](https://github.com/microsoft/hve-core/blob/main/.github/skills/project-planning/requirements-author/references/prd/ears-acceptance.md), CC BY 4.0: select a suitable sentence pattern and specify observable behavior. EARS originates with Alistair Mavin, Philip Wilkinson, Adrian Harwood, and Mark Novak (2009).\n- [Google Technical Writing One](https://developers.google.com/tech-writing/one), CC BY 4.0: write for the reader using clear structure, consistent terminology, and concise active sentences.\n\nThe guidance above is adapted and paraphrased for Watts; examples are specific to Watts.\n"}]},"isError":false}
{"type":"tool_execution_end","toolCallId":"call_34e571a5ba2a46da9904fb36","toolName":"read","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"# SPEC: Tic-tac-toe web UI\n\n## Status\nDraft\nVersion: v2\nSupersedes: none; this is the same task's draft refined at the spec step (v1 was the initial draft from the user's request)\nOwner: project user; draft refined by the specification agent\nLast updated: 2026-10-12\n\n## Intent\nAdd a minimal browser interface to the existing Go tic-tac-toe game so two people can play locally by clicking cells. This is an incremental change to the current application, not a replacement project. Preserve the terminal game while making the same rules available through a web UI.\n\n## Background and context\nThe project root (`/workspace`) already contains `go.mod` (module `tic-tac-toe`, Go 1.21), `main.go`, `main_test.go`, and an existing executable named `ttt`. Observed current behavior in `main.go`:\n\n- `board` is a 3×3 rune grid; `newBoard` starts all cells empty.\n- `playGame` runs the terminal loop over stdin/stdout with a numbered board, alternating X/O turns, and prompts after each move.\n- `parseInput` converts a single digit 1–9 to a position (0–8) and rejects empty, multi-character, and out-of-range input.\n- `checkWin` evaluates all eight winning lines (3 rows, 3 columns, 2 diagonals); `isDraw` detects a full board with no winner.\n- `main` invokes the terminal game and exits 0 on win, draw, or EOF (abandoned game).\n\n`main_test.go` establishes regression coverage for numbered rendering, all eight winning lines for both players, draw detection, input parsing boundaries, X/O wins, draws, EOF behavior, occupied-cell and invalid-input turn retention, and boards staying unchanged on rejected moves.\n\nThe team quality gates under `/inputs/resources/team/scripts/` build to private runtime storage (`build.sh` writes to `/runtime/build`), run `golangci-lint` with the pinned team config (`lint.sh`, config at `/inputs/resources/team/.golangci.yml`), and validate the spec/plan/story/verdict artifacts (`check.mjs`). The `ttt` executable must remain untouched; this task must not replace it with a container-built binary.\n\nThis task adds a loopback HTTP server and browser assets in the same module so the same game rules are reachable from a browser without changing the terminal experience.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- AC-1: When the documented web-mode Go command starts, the application shall serve the game UI and its assets locally without a frontend build step or remote CDN dependencies. Default web access shall be loopback-only, with a documented way to select the listen address.\n- AC-2: When a new game begins, the web UI shall display an empty clickable 3-by-3 board and show that X moves first. When the user selects a valid empty cell, the application shall place the current player's mark and, unless the game has ended, advance the turn to the other player.\n- AC-3: If the user selects an occupied or invalid cell, then the application shall reject the move without changing the board or the current player's turn. When either player completes a winning line or the board fills without a winner, the UI shall display the correct outcome and reject further moves until the user selects reset.\n- AC-4: When the user selects reset, the application shall clear the board, remove the previous outcome, and restore X as the current player. The UI shall remain usable on narrow/mobile viewports and shall provide readable button labels and visible turn and outcome feedback.\n- AC-5: The web interface shall reuse the existing Go game-rule functions rather than introduce a separate independent JavaScript rules engine. The terminal game and its existing tests shall continue to work, including invalid moves retaining turns, wins/draws, and EOF behavior.\n- AC-6: The change shall include tests of the new HTTP and game behavior covering valid moves and turn advancement, occupied cells, malformed or out-of-range cells, bad request payloads, reset, and completed-game handling, including moves attempted after a win or draw. The configured build, test, vet, lint, and story-completion gates shall pass without weakening checks or substituting the pinned verification tools.\n- AC-7: Application source, tests, and web assets shall live in the project root and normal source directories. Task documents and evidence shall remain in the task directory. Verification build products/caches shall remain under private runtime storage; the existing `ttt` executable shall remain unchanged. The change shall document how to run the terminal and web modes.\n\n## Explicit non-goals\n- No AI opponent, remote multiplayer, authentication, database, persistence across server restarts, deployment, or framework migration.\n- No frontend framework or JavaScript package manager/build pipeline is needed.\n- No changes to previous Watts task/eval records or runtime-owned metadata to force success.\n- No new third-party dependencies; the implementation should need only the Go standard library.\n\n## Assumptions\n- Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts, multi-game isolation, and simultaneous games in multiple tabs are not required.\n- A small standard-library Go HTTP server and local HTML/CSS/JavaScript are an appropriate implementation. Keep dependencies minimal.\n- The UI targets a modern browser with standard ES6+ and CSS layout support; no legacy-browser support is required.\n\n## Constraints\n- Preserve existing game behavior and prefer reusing concrete project functions over introducing a generic game framework.\n- Do not create a new application or module under the task directory.\n- Use the image's declared tools and quality gates. Do not install replacements or change team lint policy to suppress findings.\n- Do not read, expose, or embed provider credentials in application files or served responses.\n\n## Open questions\n- OQ-1: Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab? The v1 draft assumes one local game with no isolation but does not commit to whether the Go server or the browser page holds the board. This is observable: with server-held state a refresh resumes the same game, while with page-held state a refresh starts a new game (both are consistent with the criteria above). Recommended default: the server holds a single game's state, a refresh returns the current board, and concurrent requests are serialized. If a reviewer prefers page-held state, that should be confirmed here rather than silently chosen by the implementer.\n- No other material ambiguities were identified for the stated local-game scope. If review surfaces further ambiguity, it will be recorded here rather than turned into a requirement.\n\n## Risk-relevant context\n- Touches: local HTTP listener and request handling, browser assets served from the module, application entry point for web mode, game-state transitions shared with the terminal mode, new HTTP tests, and run documentation.\n- Does not touch: authentication, credential handling, production services, databases, deployment, external accounts, historical task evidence, or the existing `ttt` binary.\n- Requests may arrive concurrently while one game state is shared; the web handler must be race-free and must serialize state changes against the terminal game's rule functions.\n\n## Risk tier\n1\n\nRationale: the change adds a network-visible entry point and incremental game-state/logic changes on a developer machine; a mistake could silently change behavior but cannot touch identities, credentials, or entitlements, and there is no production deployment.\n\n## Review\n\nAn agent drafts this document. The declared spec-review Procedure then requires a human decision — or an explicitly delegated coordinator decision — tied to the reviewed artifact hashes. This v2 draft refines v1 against project evidence (`main.go`, `main_test.go`, `go.mod`, and the team gate scripts); no prior rejection feedback was available in this environment to incorporate. On rejection, this draft is revised in place using the feedback while preserving the original intent. A signature in this document does not approve the workflow."}]},"isError":false}
