{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":1118,"output":2506,"cacheRead":11008,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1817,"totalTokens":14632,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Good. Now I have a clear picture of the project. Let me also check if there's anything in the .golangci.yml to reference.\n\nLet me now review the current SPEC.md and plan the refinements needed.\n\nThe feedback from spec-review says:\n- Resolve OQ-1 with server-held state (one shared in-memory game, page refresh retrieves/resumes, concurrent state changes serialized, reset returns empty board with X to move)\n- Record this as a resolved choice in assumptions/open-questions section\n- Preserve existing acceptance criteria, terminal behavior, and bounded local scope\n- Do not introduce new features or requirements\n- This is a requested clarification, not an implementation failure\n\nLooking at the current SPEC.md, I notice:\n1. OQ-1 is already present in the Open Questions section - it needs to be resolved moving the answer to Assumptions\n2. The current SPEC.md already has good content from the previous agent's work\n3. There's already mention of server-held state in the current assumptions section as a recommended default\n\nLet me refine SPEC.md:\n1. Resolve OQ-1: move the resolved answer to a resolved sub-section of Open Questions, or move it to Assumptions, and update OQ-1 to indicate it's resolved\n2. Clean up the weird \"re-ordered\" text in Intent about ordering\n3. Clean up the \"Reordered intent: ...\" which seems like a note-to-self\n4. Make sure no new features are introduced\n\nLet me look more carefully at the current SPEC.md to find what needs changing.\n\nLooking at the current text:\n\n```\n- OQ-1: Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab? The v1 draft assumes one local game with no isolation but does not commit to whether the Go server or the browser page holds the board. This is observable: with server-held state a refresh resumes the same game, while with page-held state a refresh starts a new game (both are consistent with the criteria above). Recommended default: the server holds a single game's state, a refresh returns the current board, concurrent state changes are serialized, and reset returns an empty board with X to move.\n```\n\nAnd the Assumptions section already says:\n```\n- Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts, multi-game isolation, and simultaneous games in multiple tabs are not required.\n- A small standard-library Go HTTP server and local HTML/CSS/JavaScript are an appropriate implementation. Keep dependencies minimal.\n- The UI targets a modern browser with standard ES6+ and CSS layout support; no legacy-browser support is required.\n```\n\nThe feedback recommends resolving OQ-1 by committing to server-held state and recording it. Let me update the spec.\n\nAlso, I notice some odd text in the current draft:\n\n```\n- `playGame` runs the terminal loop over stdin/stdout with a numbered board, alternating X/O turns, and prompts after each move.\n```\n\nAnd then:\n\n```\nThis task adds a loopback HTTP server and browser assets in the same module so the same game rules are reachable from a browser without changing the terminal experience.\n```\n\nAlso there's a note about \"Reordered for readability\" in the constraints section that seems like a note to self, not for the final document. Let me clean up.\n\nWait, looking more carefully, the \"Reordered intent\" section seems to be embedded oddly. Let me re-read the Intent section:\n\n```\n## Intent\nAdd a minimal browser interface to the existing Go tic-tac-toe game so two people can play locally by clicking cells. This is an incremental change to the current application, not a replacement project. Preserve the terminal game while making the same rules available through a web UI.\n```\n\nThat seems fine. But then reading more of the text:\n\nThe \"Reordered for readability\" text I saw was in the Constraints section. Let me re-read:\n\n```\n## Constraints\n- Preserve existing game behavior and prefer reusing concrete project functions over introducing a generic game framework.\n- Do not create a new application or module under the task directory.\n- Use the image's declared tools and quality gates. Do not install replacements or change team lint policy to suppress findings.\n- Do not read, expose, or embed provider credentials in application files or served responses.\n```\n\nHmm, that seems fine. Let me look again at the full file more carefully.\n\nActually, I notice under Background there's a \"Reordered for readability\" comment in the bullet about `main`:\n\n```\n- `main` invokes the terminal game and exits 0 on win, draw, or EOF (abandoned game).\n```\n\nWait, I think I'm misremembering. Let me re-read the full file.\n\nLooking at the Background and context section, I see these lines:\n\n```\n`main_test.go` establishes regression coverage for numbered rendering, all eight winning lines for both players, draw detection, input parsing boundaries, X/O wins, draws, EOF behavior, occupied-cell and invalid-input turn retention, and boards staying unchanged on rejected moves.\n```\n\nThe intent and non-goals sections look good.\n\nWhat I need to change:\n1. Resolve OQ-1: move the decision to either Assumptions or resolve it in the Open Questions section, indicating it's resolved\n2. Add server-held state details to the Assumptions section\n3. The Background section already mentions \"the web handler must be race-free and must serialize state changes\" - that seems like it came from a plan-level concern. Since the feedback says \"do not introduce new features or requirements\", I should check if this belongs.\n\nActually, re-reading the feedback more carefully: \"Record this as a resolved choice in the assumptions/open-question