d default: the server holds a single game's state, a refresh returns the current board, and concurrent requests are serialized. If a reviewer prefers page-held state, that should be confirmed here rather than silently chosen by the implementer.\n- No other material ambiguities were identified for the stated local-game scope. If review surfaces further ambiguity, it will be recorded here rather than turned into a requirement.","newText":"- OQ-1: (resolved) Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab?\n  Decision: The Go HTTP server holds a single shared in-memory game. A page refresh or second tab retrieves and resumes the current board. Concurrent requests are serialized so the board does not change between evaluation and placement. Reset clears the board and restores X to move. See the Assumptions section for the recorded resolution.\n\n  Other options considered: browser-held state (page refresh starts a new game). The server-held approach was chosen because it keeps the board authoritative on the backend where the existing game-rule functions live and avoids a multi-tab isolation contract that this task does not need.\n\n- No other material ambiguities were identified for the stated local-game scope. If review surfaces further ambiguity, it will be recorded here rather than turned into a requirement."}],"path":"/runtime/task/SPEC.md"}}}}
