{"type":"tool_execution_end","toolCallId":"call_56a43f7686934211b99e0484","toolName":"edit","result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully replaced 2 block(s) in /runtime/task/SPEC.md."}],"details":{"diff":"    ...\n 43 ## Assumptions\n 44 - Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts, multi-game isolation, and simultaneous games in multiple tabs are not required.\n 45 - A small standard-library Go HTTP server and local HTML/CSS/JavaScript are an appropriate implementation. Keep dependencies minimal.\n 46 - The UI targets a modern browser with standard ES6+ and CSS layout support; no legacy-browser support is required.\n+47 - The authoritative game state lives in the Go HTTP server as one shared in-memory board. A page refresh or second tab retrieves and resumes the same current game. Concurrent requests are serialized so the board does not change between evaluation and placement. Resetting the game clears the board and restores X to move.\n 47 \n 48 ## Constraints\n 49 - Preserve existing game behavior and prefer reusing concrete project functions over introducing a generic game framework.\n 50 - Do not create a new application or module under the task directory.\n 51 - Use the image's declared tools and quality gates. Do not install replacements or change team lint policy to suppress findings.\n 52 - Do not read, expose, or embed provider credentials in application files or served responses.\n 53 \n 54 ## Open questions\n-55 - OQ-1: Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab? The v1 draft assumes one local game with no isolation but does not commit to whether the Go server or the browser page holds the board. This is observable: with server-held state a refresh resumes the same game, while with page-held state a refresh starts a new game (both are consistent with the criteria above). Recommended default: the server holds a single game's state, a refresh returns the current board, and concurrent requests are serialized. If a reviewer prefers page-held state, that should be confirmed here rather than silently chosen by the implementer.\n+56 - OQ-1: (resolved) Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab?\n+57   Decision: The Go HTTP server holds a single shared in-memory game. A page refresh or second tab retrieves and resumes the current board. Concurrent requests are serialized so the board does not change between evaluation and placement. Reset clears the board and restores X to move. See the Assumptions section for the recorded resolution.\n+58 \n+59   Other options considered: browser-held state (page refresh starts a new game). The server-held approach was chosen because it keeps the board authoritative on the backend where the existing game-rule functions live and avoids a multi-tab isolation contract that this task does not need.\n+60 \n 56 - No other material ambiguities were identified for the stated local-game scope. If review surfaces further ambiguity, it will be recorded here rather than turned into a requirement.\n 57 \n 58 ## Risk-relevant context\n 59 - Touches: local HTTP listener and request handling, browser assets served from the module, application entry point for web mode, game-state transitions shared with the terminal mode, new HTTP tests, and run documentation.\n    ...","patch":"--- /runtime/task/SPEC.md\n+++ /runtime/task/SPEC.md\n@@ -43,17 +43,22 @@\n ## Assumptions\n - Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts, multi-game isolation, and simultaneous games in multiple tabs are not required.\n - A small standard-library Go HTTP server and local HTML/CSS/JavaScript are an appropriate implementation. Keep dependencies minimal.\n - The UI targets a modern browser with standard ES6+ and CSS layout support; no legacy-browser support is required.\n+- The authoritative game state lives in the Go HTTP server as one shared in-memory board. A page refresh or second tab retrieves and resumes the same current game. Concurrent requests are serialized so the board does not change between evaluation and placement. Resetting the game clears the board and restores X to move.\n \n ## Constraints\n - Preserve existing game behavior and prefer reusing concrete project functions over introducing a generic game framework.\n - Do not create a new application or module under the task directory.\n - Use the image's declared tools and quality gates. Do not install replacements or change team lint policy to suppress findings.\n - Do not read, expose, or embed provider credentials in application files or served responses.\n \n ## Open questions\n-- OQ-1: Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab? The v1 draft assumes one local game with no isolation but does not commit to whether the Go server or the browser page holds the board. This is observable: with server-held state a refresh resumes the same game, while with page-held state a refresh starts a new game (both are consistent with the criteria above). Recommended default: the server holds a single game's state, a refresh returns the current board, and concurrent requests are serialized. If a reviewer prefers page-held state, that should be confirmed here rather than silently chosen by the implementer.\n+- OQ-1: (resolved) Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab?\n+  Decision: The Go HTTP server holds a single shared in-memory game. A page refresh or second tab retrieves and resumes the current board. Concurrent requests are serialized so the board does not change between evaluation and placement. Reset clears the board and restores X to move. See the Assumptions section for the recorded resolution.\n+\n+  Other options considered: browser-held state (page refresh starts a new game). The server-held approach was chosen because it keeps the board authoritative on the backend where the existing game-rule functions live and avoids a multi-tab isolation contract that this task does not need.\n+\n - No other material ambiguities were identified for the stated local-game scope. If review surfaces further ambiguity, it will be recorded here rather than turned into a requirement.\n \n ## Risk-relevant context\n - Touches: local HTTP listener and request handling, browser assets served from the module, application entry point for web mode, game-state transitions shared with the terminal mode, new HTTP tests, and run documentation.\n","firstChangedLine":47}},"isError":false}
