k\\n3. There's already mention of server-held state in the current assumptions section as a recommended default\\n\\nLet me refine SPEC.md:\\n1. Resolve OQ-1: move the resolved answer to a resolved sub-section of Open Questions, or move it to Assumptions, and update OQ-1 to indicate it's resolved\\n2. Clean up the weird \\\"re-ordered\\\" text in Intent about ordering\\n3. Clean up the \\\"Reordered intent: ...\\\" which seems like a note-to-self\\n4. Make sure no new features are introduced\\n\\nLet me look more carefully at the current SPEC.md to find what needs changing.\\n\\nLooking at the current text:\\n\\n```\\n- OQ-1: Where should the authoritative game state live, and what should happen after a page refresh or in a second tab? The v1 draft assumes one local game with no isolation but does not commit to whether the Go server or the browser page holds the board. This is observable: with server-held state a refresh resumes the same game, while with page-held state a refresh starts a new game (both are consistent with the criteria above). Recommended default: the server holds a single game's state, a refresh returns the current board, concurrent state changes are serialized, and reset returns an empty board with X to move.\\n```\\n\\nAnd the Assumptions section already says:\\n```\\n- Two humans play one local game, alternating at the browser; independent accounts, multi-game isolation, and simultaneous games in multiple tabs are not required.\\n- A small standard-library Go HTTP server and local HTML/CSS/JavaScript are an appropriate implementation. Keep dependencies minimal.\\n- The UI targets a modern browser with standard ES6+ and CSS layout support; no legacy-browser support is required.\\n```\\n\\nThe feedback recommends resolving OQ-1 by committing to server-held state and recording it. Let me update the spec.\\n\\nAlso, I notice some odd text in the current draft:\\n\\n```\\n- `playGame` runs the terminal loop over stdin/stdout with a numbered board, al