horitative game state lives in a single in-memory `gameState` struct behind a `sync.Mutex` in the Go HTTP server. The browser is a stateless view. A page refresh or second tab retrieves state via `GET /state` and renders it. This is consistent with the resolved OQ-1 decision in SPEC.md.\n101:- **Rejected**: Client-only state — a page refresh would lose the game, violating the server-held state requirement.\n113:- `TestGetState_Initial` — `GET /state` returns empty board, X to move, no winner, not game over.\n114:- `TestGetState_AfterMoves` — Make a valid move via `POST /move`, then `GET /state` returns the updated board with correct current player.\n115:- `TestGetState_RefreshHydration` — Simulate a second request (as from a refresh or second tab) and verify the state is identical (not reset).\n116:- `TestGetState_AfterFailedRequest` — Send a malformed `POST /move` (rejected), then `GET /state` returns the original board unchanged.\n125:- `TestGetState_NoResetOnRefresh` — Verify multiple `GET /state` calls in sequence return the same state without mutating it.\n191:### US-001: Web mode startup with state retrieval\n203:- [ ] `GET /state` returns `200 OK` with JSON containing an empty 3×3 board, `currentPlayer: \"X\"`, `winner: \"\"`, `gameOver: false`.\n204:- [ ] After a `POST /move` places a mark, `GET /state` returns the updated board with the correct current player. Multiple sequential `GET /state` calls return identical state (no mutation).\n205:- [ ] After a failed request (e.g., malformed payload), `GET /state` returns the unchanged prior board.\n294:None. SPEC.md's resolved OQ-1 (server-held state with GET /state hydration) is addressed by the state retrieval endpoint and the stories above. No remaining ambiguities.\n"}],"details":{}}}
